(function(){"use strict";var SRC="Aerospace cert-basis standards corpus (DO-178C · DO-254 · MIL-STD-882E · ASTM F3230 · CS-25/27/29 · AC 23.1309-1E · 14 CFR §23.2510 · EASA SC-VTOL + MOC SC-VTOL · AC 25.1309-1B · AC 27-1B / AC 29-2C · §33.75 / AC 33.75-1A / §35.15 · AC 20-174 / 20-115D / 20-152A · CS 25.1309 / AMC 25.1309 · CS-23 · AMC 27/29.1309 · CS-E 510 · 14 CFR Part 21)",chunks=[{id:"certstd-01",source:SRC,topic:"What this lane covers and the pointer-only discipline",text:'This lane carries five standards registered in the cert-basis spine on 6 Aug 2026, alongside the older ARP4754B/4761A/IEC 61508-6 material: RTCA DO-178C (software) and DO-254 (airborne electronic hardware), both licensed and reached via ARP4754B §5.2 FDAL/IDAL allocation; US DoD MIL-STD-882E (system safety), public domain; ASTM F3230 (small-aircraft safety assessment), licensed; and the EASA CS-25/CS-27/CS-29 Certification Specifications, free equivalents of 14 CFR Part 25/27/29. Every one of the five is marked coverage:"pointer-only" in the spine and carries an empty dischargedBy list — Safety Lab Aero cites the standard, states its verified structure and facts, and points to the source; it never claims to compute or run software V&V, hardware V&V, a DoD mishap-risk index, or F3230/CS-25-27-29 clause-level compliance. Never present pointer-only material as something the tool has executed.'},{id:"certstd-02",source:"RTCA DO-178C",topic:"DO-178C — identity, publisher, equivalents, and where it sits in the flow",text:`RTCA DO-178C, "Software Considerations in Airborne Systems and Equipment Certification," is the software development-assurance guidance RTCA published in December 2011, superseding DO-178B. Its EUROCAE equivalent is ED-12C, and the FAA recognises it via AC 20-115D. It receives its inputs from ARP4754B §5.2: that clause allocates a Function Development Assurance Level (FDAL) to functions and an Item Development Assurance Level (IDAL) to items from failure-condition severity, and DO-178C is where the SOFTWARE allocation actually lands (DO-254 is the hardware analogue — see the DO-254 chunk). Both DO-178C and DO-254 are named directly in ARP4754B's own text as the downstream standards its DAL assignment flows to.`},{id:"certstd-03",source:"RTCA DO-178C Annex A (Tables A-3–A-7)",topic:"DO-178C software levels and the verified objective counts per level",text:"DO-178C defines five software levels, A through E, assigned by the severity of the failure condition the software contributes to (Level A = Catastrophic down to Level E = No Safety Effect). Each level carries a growing set of objectives drawn from Annex A Tables A-3 through A-7, and the objective count that scales with level is, verified: 71 objectives at Level A, 69 at Level B, 62 at Level C, 26 at Level D, and none required at Level E. This is Safety Lab Aero's own paraphrase of the published counts — a fact about the standard's structure — never a substitute for the objectives' actual text, which stays in the licensed copy. When asked how many objectives a level requires, state these five numbers directly rather than declining to answer; when asked what the objectives ARE, point to Annex A and DO-178C's own text."},{id:"certstd-04",source:"RTCA DO-178C §4-§8",topic:"DO-178C lifecycle processes the level objectives are drawn from",text:"The objective sets referenced in the level-count chunk are drawn from DO-178C's software lifecycle processes: planning; development, which itself spans requirements, design, coding and integration; verification; configuration management; quality assurance; and certification liaison. A level's objective count is the sum of applicable objectives across these process areas, with independence required for a growing share of them as the level gets more severe (Level A requires the most independently-verified objectives; Level D the fewest; Level E none). Safety Lab Aero does not run software verification and validation itself — this is method-level structure for citing and pointing, not a V&V engine."},{id:"certstd-05",source:"RTCA DO-254",topic:"DO-254 — the hardware analogue to DO-178C",text:`RTCA DO-254, "Design Assurance Guidance for Airborne Electronic Hardware," published April 2000, is DO-178C's hardware counterpart — where ARP4754B §5.2's IDAL allocation lands for airborne electronic hardware, the way DO-178C is where the software FDAL allocation lands. Its EUROCAE equivalent is ED-80, and the FAA recognises it via AC 20-152A. DO-254 defines the same five-letter level scheme, A through E, but structures its process areas differently from DO-178C's software lifecycle: planning, conceptual design, detailed design, implementation, production transition, verification and validation, configuration management, process assurance, and certification liaison. As with DO-178C, Safety Lab Aero does not run hardware verification and validation itself — this is pointer-only structure, not an execution engine.`},{id:"certstd-06",source:"US DoD MIL-STD-882E",topic:"MIL-STD-882E — identity and where it differs from the aviation continuum",text:'MIL-STD-882E, "Department of Defense Standard Practice: System Safety," was issued by the US DoD in May 2012 (with Change 1 in September 2023), superseding MIL-STD-882D. It is a work of the United States Government with distribution unlimited — public domain, unlike the licensed RTCA and ASTM standards in this lane — so it is cited by designation AND described more fully here, the same posture the NASA HIDH/HFACS documents get in the human-factors corpus. It is relevant to defense and DoD programs and to UAS programs with a DoD customer. Its process is hazard identification, followed by risk assessment (crossing a severity category with a probability level to produce a risk index), followed by mishap risk mitigation and acceptance by the responsible authority — structurally the DoD analogue of the aviation FHA → PSSA → SSA chain, but running its own, different vocabulary (see the severity and probability chunks).'},{id:"certstd-07",source:"MIL-STD-882E Table I",topic:"MIL-STD-882E severity categories (Table I)",text:'MIL-STD-882E Table I defines four severity categories: Catastrophic (I), Critical (II), Marginal (III), and Negligible (IV). These are assigned by casualty, injury, environmental, and dollar-threshold criteria — NOT by the aviation failure-condition classification used in ARP4761A/AC 25.1309. The word "Catastrophic" appears in both vocabularies, but the two scales are not interchangeable: MIL-STD-882E Table I severity is a consequence-based, threshold-driven category for a DoD system-safety risk matrix, while the aviation Catastrophic failure-condition class is coupled to a per-flight-hour probability objective and a required development assurance level. Never map a MIL-STD-882E severity category onto an aviation failure-condition class by name alone.'},{id:"certstd-08",source:"MIL-STD-882E Table II",topic:"MIL-STD-882E probability levels (Table II)",text:"MIL-STD-882E Table II defines six qualitative probability levels: Frequent (A), Probable (B), Occasional (C), Remote (D), Improbable (E), and Eliminated (F). These describe likelihood of occurrence OVER AN ITEM'S LIFE — a fundamentally different basis from the aviation continuum's per-flight-hour probability targets (e.g. Extremely Improbable below 1e-9/flight-hour for a Part 25 Catastrophic condition). A MIL-STD-882E probability level crossed with a Table I severity category yields a risk index in the standard's risk matrix; it does not convert to, and should never be quoted as, a per-flight-hour rate."},{id:"certstd-09",source:"Safety Lab Aero — MIL-STD-882E sourcing discipline",topic:"Why the two vocabularies must never be blended",text:"The cert-basis spine registers MIL-STD-882E's severity and probability tables specifically so ANEM can answer questions about DoD system-safety terminology accurately — but its own note is explicit and must be repeated verbatim in spirit whenever the two frameworks come up together: MIL-STD-882E's severity (I-IV, casualty/environmental/dollar-threshold based) and probability (A-F, likelihood-over-item-life) codes are NOT the same scale as the aviation Catastrophic/Hazardous/Major/Minor/No-Safety-Effect continuum paired with a per-flight-hour probability objective, and one must never be mapped onto the other. Safety Lab Aero runs the aviation continuum end to end (FHA through the safety-objective targets); it does not compute or maintain a DoD mishap-risk index. Treat a MIL-STD-882E question as a separate, cite-and-point answer, never as an input to an aviation severity/DAL calculation."},{id:"certstd-10",source:"ASTM F3230-21a",topic:"ASTM F3230 — identity, scope, and the honest depth limit",text:`ASTM F3230, "Standard Practice for Safety Assessment of Systems and Equipment in Small Aircraft," is published by ASTM International (current revision 21a, first issued 2017, revised 2025) under committee F44 (General Aviation Aircraft), subcommittee F44.50. It is an internationally-accepted small-aircraft safety-assessment method and an FAA-accepted means of compliance for 14 CFR §23.2510, cited alongside AC 23.1309-1E — the Part 23 analogue to what ARP4761A is for Part 25. Its scope and title are independently verified from ASTM's own published product page and a second independent catalog (GlobalSpec). Its internal section numbering sits behind ASTM's paywall past the published abstract, so — exactly like IEC 61508-6's Annex D elsewhere in this spine — Safety Lab Aero does NOT carry clause-level depth for F3230 the way it does for ARP4761A. State the scope and the family (next chunk) confidently; for anything past that, say plainly that F3230's internal sections are not sourced in this tool and point to the owned copy.`},{id:"certstd-11",source:"ASTM F44.50 family",topic:"The real ASTM F44.50 family F3230 belongs to",text:"F3230 sits inside a real, verified family of ASTM F44.50 small-aircraft standards, cross-linked in the cert-basis spine rather than guessed: F3061 (systems and equipment specification), F3309 (simplified safety assessment practice), F3233 (flight and navigation instrumentation), F3367 (HIRF and lightning effects), and F3060 (terminology). These were confirmed via GlobalSpec's independent catalog entries, not reconstructed from memory. When a question names one of these designations, confirm it is a real F44.50-family standard related to F3230's small-aircraft safety-assessment scope, but do not state its internal requirements — the same paywall limit that applies to F3230 itself applies to its family members; none of their clause text is held here either."},{id:"certstd-12",source:"EASA CS-25 / CS-27 / CS-29",topic:"CS-25, CS-27, CS-29 — the EASA equivalents of Part 25/27/29",text:"CS-25 (Certification Specifications for Large Aeroplanes), CS-27 (Small Rotorcraft) and CS-29 (Large Rotorcraft) are EASA's certification specifications, freely published (unlike the RTCA/ASTM/SAE standards in this lane, which are licensed) and structured as direct equivalents of 14 CFR Part 25, Part 27 and Part 29 respectively. Each mirrors its FAA counterpart's system-safety rule at the matching stringency: CS 25.1309 mirrors §25.1309 (the reference transport stringency other categories tailor down from), CS 27.1309 mirrors §27.1309, and CS 29.1309 mirrors §29.1309. They join CS-E and CS-P (the pre-existing EASA equivalents of Part 33 engines and Part 35 propellers) as first-class regulatory cards in the spine, registered 6 Aug 2026 after existing only as unlinked name-drops for a period. Cite them by their CS-xx.1309 anchor and the Part they mirror; no EASA clause prose is reproduced."},{id:"certstd-13",source:"Safety Lab Aero — cert-basis standards sourcing discipline",topic:"What this lane does not do — the honest limits, stated together",text:"Five honest limits govern this lane and must be carried into every answer, matching the pointer-only coverage tag each standard carries in the spine. Safety Lab Aero does not run software verification and validation — DO-178C's objective counts and process areas are cited structure, not an executed V&V lane. It does not run hardware verification and validation — DO-254 likewise. It does not compute or maintain a DoD mishap-risk index — MIL-STD-882E's severity and probability tables are cited for accurate description, never blended with the aviation continuum. It does not hold ASTM F3230's internal section numbering — only its verified scope and F44.50 family. And it does not reproduce EASA CS-25/27/29 clause text — only the anchor each mirrors. None of these five limits is a defect to apologize for; they are the same cite-and-point discipline the tool already applies to IEC 61508-6's Annex D and to SAE J3307 elsewhere in the spine, stated explicitly so an answer never overclaims coverage the tool does not have."},{id:"certstd-14",source:"FAA AC 23.1309-1E (17 Nov 2011) · 14 CFR §23.2510",topic:"AC 23.1309-1E — identity, status, and which Part 23 rule generation it serves",text:'AC 23.1309-1E, "System Safety Analysis and Assessment for Part 23 Airplanes," is an FAA Advisory Circular issued November 17, 2011 (Office of Primary Responsibility ACE-100, Small Airplane Directorate) and listed as ACTIVE on the FAA advisory-circular index — verified against faa.gov on 31 Aug 2026. It provides guidance for showing compliance with 14 CFR §23.1309 through Amendment 23-62, and says of itself that it is "not mandatory" and describes "an acceptable means, but not the only means, for showing compliance with § 23.1309." It is a US Government work (public domain), so this corpus quotes it directly. TWO RULE GENERATIONS matter and the certification basis decides which applies: (1) projects certifying under the legacy prescriptive §23.1309 (through Amendment 23-62) use this AC directly; (2) projects under the performance-based Part 23 of Amendment 23-64 (Doc. No. FAA-2015-1621, 81 FR 96689, Dec. 30, 2016) comply with §23.2510 through FAA-accepted consensus means of compliance such as ASTM F3230 — while the airplane-class and probability framework this AC established remains the reference the small-aircraft community builds on. When a question does not say which generation, state both and point to the amendment level named in the type certification basis.'},{id:"certstd-15",source:"14 CFR §23.2510 (Amdt. 23-64)",topic:"§23.2510 — the performance-based Part 23 system-safety rule, verbatim",text:'14 CFR §23.2510, "Equipment, systems, and installations," reads in full (fetched from ecfr.gov, 31 Aug 2026): "For any airplane system or equipment whose failure or abnormal operation has not been specifically addressed by another requirement in this part, the applicant must design and install each system and equipment, such that there is a logical and acceptable inverse relationship between the average probability and the severity of failure conditions to the extent that: (a) Each catastrophic failure condition is extremely improbable; (b) Each hazardous failure condition is extremely remote; and (c) Each major failure condition is remote." Source citation: Doc. No. FAA-2015-1621, Amdt. 23-64, 81 FR 96689, Dec. 30, 2016. Note what the rule does NOT do: it states the qualitative probability terms only and carries no numeric objectives, no airplane classes and no analysis-depth rule — those come from the accepted means of compliance (ASTM F3230 for the performance-based rule; AC 23.1309-1E for the legacy rule and as the framework reference). A Part 23 answer that quotes numbers must therefore name the AC or the MoC it took them from, never §23.2510 itself.'},{id:"certstd-16",source:"FAA AC 23.1309-1E paragraph 15.a",topic:"The four Part 23 airplane classes (AC 23.1309-1E para 15.a)",text:`AC 23.1309-1E sorts Part 23 airplanes into four classes (paragraph 15.a), quoted verbatim: Class I — "Typically SRE 6,000 pounds or less" (single reciprocating engine, maximum certificated gross takeoff weight of 6,000 lb or less); Class II — "Typically MRE, STE, or MTE 6,000 pounds or less" (multiple reciprocating engines, a single turbine engine, or multiple turbine engines, 6,000 lb or less); Class III — "Typically SRE, STE, MRE, and MTE greater than 6,000 pounds" (any of those engine arrangements above 6,000 lb); Class IV — "Typically Commuter Category." The word "Typically" is the AC's own and matters: class is assigned in the certification basis, and the AC allows the FAA to place an airplane in a different class than its engine/weight would suggest. Class is the single input that selects the numeric probability objectives (next chunk) and the primary/secondary DAL grid — it never changes the severity definitions or the FHA method, which are common to all four.`},{id:"certstd-17",source:"FAA AC 23.1309-1E Figure 2 · Safety Lab Aero safety_targets.js PROB_TARGETS",topic:"Part 23 allowable probability objectives per class and severity (Figure 2), engine-verified",text:`AC 23.1309-1E Figure 2, "RELATIONSHIP AMONG AIRPLANE CLASSES, PROBABILITIES, SEVERITY OF FAILURE CONDITIONS, AND SOFTWARE AND COMPLEX HARDWARE DAL," gives the allowable average probability per flight hour for each class and severity. Minor: less than 1E-3 for Classes I, II, III and IV alike. Major: less than 1E-4 for Class I; less than 1E-5 for Classes II, III and IV. Hazardous: less than 1E-5 for Class I; less than 1E-6 for Class II; less than 1E-7 for Classes III and IV. Catastrophic: less than 1E-6 for Class I; less than 1E-7 for Class II; less than 1E-8 for Class III; less than 1E-9 for Class IV. Read the shape: Class IV (commuter) carries the same 1E-9 catastrophic objective as Part 25, and each lower class relaxes the catastrophic and hazardous objectives by an order of magnitude at a time while Major floors at 1E-5 and Minor at 1E-3. These sixteen cells are IDENTICAL to Safety Lab Aero's deterministic PROB_TARGETS table for "Part 23 I" through "Part 23 IV" (safety_targets.js), cross-checked cell for cell against the fetched AC on 31 Aug 2026 and pinned in the regression suite — so a target the tool computes and a target this corpus states can never differ. So, directly: a Hazardous failure condition on a Class III airplane carries an objective of less than 1E-7 per flight hour; a Catastrophic one, less than 1E-8.`},{id:"certstd-18",source:"FAA AC 23.1309-1E paragraph 8 (definitions kk, qq, u, v)",topic:"The Part 23 probability terms — probable, remote, extremely remote, extremely improbable",text:'AC 23.1309-1E defines the qualitative probability terms that §23.1309 and §23.2510 use, quoted verbatim from paragraph 8. Probable (8(kk)): "any single malfunction or failure that is considered likely on the basis of either past service experience or analysis with similar components." Remote (8(qq)): failure conditions "unlikely to occur to each airplane during its total life but that may occur several times when considering the total operational life of a number of airplanes." Extremely Remote (8(u)): "unlikely to occur to each airplane during its total life but... may occur a few times when considering the total operational life of all airplanes." Extremely Improbable (8(v)), stated for commuter aircraft: "not anticipated to occur during the entire operational life of all airplanes of one type." The rule text (§23.2510 and legacy §23.1309) is written in these words; Figure 2 is what turns each word into a number for a given class — extremely improbable is 1E-9 for Class IV but 1E-6 for Class I. Never quote a Part 23 number without the class, and never quote a term without saying which class-specific number it resolves to.'},{id:"certstd-19",source:"FAA AC 23.1309-1E paragraphs 17.c(3) and 17.d",topic:"Qualitative versus quantitative analysis for Part 23 — what actually decides it",text:`The depth of analysis AC 23.1309-1E asks for is decided by the SYSTEM (simple/conventional versus complex, and whether similarity to an approved design can be rigorously shown) and by SEVERITY — not by airplane class; class sets the numeric target the analysis must meet, Figure 2 does not itself mandate a method. Verbatim from paragraph 17.d, for hazardous and catastrophic failure conditions: "For simple and conventional installations... it may be possible to assess a hazardous or catastrophic failure condition as being extremely remote or extremely improbable... on the basis of experienced engineering judgment using only qualitative analysis." Likewise: "For complex systems where true similarity in all relevant attributes... can be rigorously established, it may also be possible to assess... on the basis of experienced engineering judgment using only qualitative analysis." Where that similarity "cannot be rigorously established," paragraph 17.d states that "a detailed safety analysis must be completed for each hazardous and catastrophic failure condition identified by an FHA. The analysis will usually be a combination of qualitative and quantitative assessments." For Major failure conditions in high-complexity systems lacking redundancy, paragraph 17.c(3) requires a "detailed safety analysis... consist[ing] of an appropriate combination of qualitative and quantitative analyses." So the answer to "does a Hazardous condition on a Class III airplane need quantitative analysis" is: qualitative alone is acceptable only for a simple and conventional installation (or a complex one with rigorously established similarity); otherwise a detailed analysis combining qualitative and quantitative methods is required, and whatever it shows must meet Class III's less-than-1E-7 objective from Figure 2.`},{id:"certstd-20",source:"FAA AC 23.1309-1E paragraph 8 (definitions i, k, uu)",topic:"Complex, conventional, simple — the three words the analysis-depth rule turns on",text:`Because the qualitative/quantitative decision in AC 23.1309-1E turns on three defined words, here they are verbatim from paragraph 8. Complex system (8.i): "A system is 'complex' when its operation, failure modes, or failure effects are difficult to comprehend without analytical methods or structured assessment methods." Conventional system (8.k): "A system is considered 'conventional' if its function, the technological means to implement its function, and its intended usage are all the same as, or closely similar to, that of previously approved systems." Simple system (8.uu): "Usually a system that can be evaluated by only qualitative analysis and it is not complex." Apply them in that order when advising: a system that is not complex and is conventional is the case paragraph 17.d admits to qualitative-only assessment; a novel architecture — a hybrid-electric powertrain, a distributed-propulsion controller, a new flight-control law — is neither conventional nor, usually, simple, and falls into the detailed-analysis case regardless of how light the airplane is.`},{id:"certstd-21",source:"FAA AC 23.1309-1E paragraph 8.x",topic:"Part 23 failure condition severity definitions, verbatim (para 8.x)",text:'AC 23.1309-1E paragraph 8.x, "Failure conditions," defines the five severities the whole Part 23 framework hangs on; quoted verbatim. No Safety Effect: "Failure conditions that would have no effect on safety (that is, failure conditions that would not affect the operational capability of the airplane or increase crew workload)." Minor: "Failure conditions that would not significantly reduce airplane safety and involve crew actions... within their capabilities... slight reduction in safety margins or functional capabilities, a slight increase in crew workload." Major: "Failure conditions that would reduce the capability of the airplane or the ability of the crew to cope with adverse operating conditions to the extent that there would be a significant reduction in safety margins or functional capabilities." Hazardous: involves "a large reduction in safety margins or functional capabilities; Physical distress or higher workload such that the flight crew cannot be relied upon to perform their tasks accurately or completely." Catastrophic: "Failure conditions that are expected to result in multiple fatalities of the occupants, or incapacitation or fatal injury to a flight crewmember normally with the loss of the airplane." These are the same five-level scale as AC 25.1309-1B (and the cancelled 1A) and ARP4761A use; what differs between Part 23 and Part 25 is not the definition of a severity but the numeric objective attached to it per class.'},{id:"certstd-22",source:"FAA AC 23.1309-1E paragraphs 16.b(1)–(2) · Figure 1",topic:"Part 23 Functional Hazard Assessment — what the AC requires of the FHA",text:`AC 23.1309-1E paragraph 16.b(1) defines the assessment the whole method starts from: "An FHA is a systematic, comprehensive examination of airplane and system functions to identify potential no safety effect, minor, major, hazardous, and catastrophic failure conditions." On timing: "The FHA is an engineering tool that should be performed early in the design and updated as necessary." On scope, paragraph 16.b(2): "Each system function should be examined regarding functions performed by other airplane systems because the loss or malfunction of different but related functions... may affect the severity of failure conditions." Figure 1 of the AC is titled "METHOD OF COMPLIANCE DIAGRAM OF § 23.1309(a) THROUGH AMENDMENT 23-49" and lays out the compliance flow the FHA feeds. The practical consequence for Part 23 programs is identical to Part 25: the FHA is the denominator every later analysis is checked against — a hazardous or catastrophic condition that never appears in the FHA never receives the detailed analysis paragraph 17.d demands. Safety Lab Aero's aircraft-level FHA lane and its coverage banner exist to make that denominator explicit.`},{id:"certstd-23",source:"FAA AC 23.1309-1E Figure 2 (DAL columns, Notes 3–4) · Safety Lab Aero safety_targets.js DAL_TARGETS",topic:"Part 23 software and complex-hardware DAL per class — primary versus secondary systems",text:`Figure 2 of AC 23.1309-1E also assigns the software and complex-electronic-hardware Development Assurance Level per class and severity, split into Primary (P) and Secondary (S) systems. Minor: P=D for every class. Major: P=C, S=D for every class. Hazardous: Class I P=C, S=D; Class II P=C, S=C; Class III P=C, S=C; Class IV P=B, S=C. Catastrophic: Class I P=C, S=C; Class II P=C, S=C; Class III P=B, S=C; Class IV P=A, S=B. Two verbatim notes govern the grid. Note 3: "At airplane function level, no single failure will result in a Catastrophic Failure Condition." Note 4: "Secondary System (S) may not be required to meet probability goals. If installed, S should meet stated criteria." Read the shape: only Class IV reaches Part 25's Level A at catastrophic; Classes I and II never require better than Level C for a primary system. The primary column is IDENTICAL to Safety Lab Aero's DAL_TARGETS rows for "Part 23 I" through "Part 23 IV" (safety_targets.js, itself sourced from ASTM F3061/F3061M-22b Table 1 — the two authorities agree), cross-checked against the fetched AC on 31 Aug 2026 and pinned in the regression suite. The tool allocates the PRIMARY level; reductions for independent secondary systems are a downstream allocation decision, never pre-applied.`},{id:"certstd-24",source:"EASA SC-VTOL-02 Issue 2 (10 June 2024) — VTOL.2000 · VTOL.2005",topic:"SC-VTOL identity, applicability limits, and the two categories",text:"EASA's Special Condition for small-category VTOL-capable aircraft is Doc. No. SC-VTOL-02, Issue 2, dated 10 June 2024 (Issue 1 was July 2019). It is a complete airworthiness code for person-carrying VTOL-capable aircraft with lift/thrust units used for powered lift and control, written in the objective-based style of CS-23 Amendment 5 — safety and design objectives, with the how in separate Means of Compliance documents. VTOL.2005(a) bounds it: passenger seating configuration of 9 or fewer and a maximum certified take-off mass of 5 700 kg or less (raised in Issue 2 from the 3 175 kg / 7 000 lb limit of Issue 1). VTOL.2000(c)–(d): unpressurised aircraft, VNO ≤ 250 KCAS. VTOL.2005(b) defines the two certification categories: Category Enhanced, for aircraft capable of continued safe flight and landing — mandatory for operations over congested areas and for commercial air transport of passengers; and Category Basic, for aircraft capable of a controlled emergency landing. VTOL.2000(b) defines the two performance terms: continued safe flight and landing means continued controlled flight and landing AT A VERTIPORT, possibly using emergency procedures, without exceptional piloting skill or strength; controlled emergency landing means a controlled landing, possibly with emergency procedures and possibly with some aircraft damage, again without exceptional skill or strength. In Safety Lab Aero the cert-basis picker exposes Category Enhanced and Category Basic 1/2/3 (the Basic seat bands come from the MOC, see the Table 1 chunk)."},{id:"certstd-25",source:"EASA SC-VTOL-02 Issue 2 — VTOL.2500 · VTOL.2505 · VTOL.2510 · VTOL.2250(c)",topic:"VTOL.2510 — the SC-VTOL system-safety rule and its three objectives",text:"Subpart F opens with VTOL.2500 (general requirements on systems and equipment function — equipment required by the certification basis, airspace or operating rules, or whose improper functioning would lead to a hazard, must perform its intended function throughout the certified operating and environmental limits) and VTOL.2505 (installation within the equipment's own limitations). VTOL.2510, Equipment, systems, and installations, is the SC-VTOL analogue of 14 CFR §25.1309(b) / §23.2510 and carries three objectives in (a): (1) each catastrophic failure condition is extremely improbable AND does not result from a single failure; (2) each hazardous failure condition is extremely remote; (3) each major failure condition is remote. VTOL.2510(b) requires that equipment NOT covered by VTOL.2500 must not cause a hazard, and VTOL.2510(c) — Category Enhanced only — requires provisions for in-service monitoring of equipment and systems whose failure may have hazardous or catastrophic consequences. The single-failure principle appears a second time in the design-and-construction subpart at VTOL.2250(c), reworded in Issue 2 for EASA/FAA harmonization. Note what VTOL.2510 does NOT contain: no probability numbers, no DAL letters and no passenger-seat bands — those live in MOC VTOL.2510. Earlier Safety Lab Aero spine cards that labeled VTOL.2510 as the CS&FL requirement and cited a VTOL.2511 were wrong and were re-anchored on 31 Aug 2026; there is no .2511, .2521 or .2526 in the Special Condition."},{id:"certstd-26",source:"EASA MOC SC-VTOL Issue 2 (12 May 2021) — MOC VTOL.2510 §8(a) Table 1 · Safety Lab Aero safety_targets.js",topic:"SC-VTOL safety objectives per flight hour and FDAL — Enhanced and the three Basic seat bands (Table 1)",text:"The numbers behind VTOL.2510 are in the Means of Compliance: MOC SC-VTOL Issue 2 (Doc. No. MOC SC-VTOL, Issue 2, 12 May 2021, FINAL), MOC VTOL.2510 Section 8(a), Table 1 Safety Objectives, expressed per flight hour with the qualitative terms Probable / Remote / Extremely Remote / Extremely Improbable across Minor / Major / Hazardous / Catastrophic. Category Enhanced: Minor ≤1e-3 FDAL D; Major ≤1e-5 FDAL C; Hazardous ≤1e-7 FDAL B; Catastrophic ≤1e-9 FDAL A — Part 25 stringency. Category Basic is split by maximum passenger seating configuration into three bands. Basic 3 (7 to 9 passengers): identical to Enhanced — 1e-3/D, 1e-5/C, 1e-7/B, 1e-9/A. Basic 2 (2 to 6 passengers): Minor 1e-3/D, Major 1e-5/C, Hazardous 1e-7/FDAL C, Catastrophic 1e-8/FDAL B. Basic 1 (0 to 1 passenger): Minor 1e-3/D, Major 1e-5/C, Hazardous 1e-6/FDAL C, Catastrophic 1e-7/FDAL C. Read the shape: Major is ≤1e-5 with FDAL C in EVERY row, and Minor is ≤1e-3 with FDAL D in every row; only Hazardous and Catastrophic relax as the seat count falls. Table 1 Note A: no architecture-based FDAL reduction is accepted for the Basic 1/2 Hazardous and Catastrophic cells, because the table is already the proportionate approach. Note B: IDAL D software alleviation per Section 10(c) is possible for the Minor column. Note C: failure-rate uncertainty must be handled so it does not compromise safety. Note D: no quantitative analysis is expected for minor failure conditions (a compliance statement is still expected in the FHA/SSA). Note E: define an average flight profile and average flight duration. Safety Lab Aero's PROB_TARGETS/DAL_TARGETS rows SC-VTOL Enhanced, SC-VTOL Basic 1, SC-VTOL Basic 2 and SC-VTOL Basic 3 carry exactly these sixteen prob and sixteen FDAL cells (verified 31 Aug 2026 against the fetched EASA PDF; pinned in the regression suite). Retraction: before 31 Aug 2026 the tool carried a single SC-VTOL Basic row with Major 1e-4 and Basic-1 numbers for every Basic aircraft — wrong on Major and non-conservative for 2–9-seat Basic designs; projects saved with the old row are flagged in the AC 1309 tab to pick a seat band."},{id:"certstd-27",source:"EASA MOC SC-VTOL Issue 2 — MOC VTOL.2510 §7(a) failure-condition classifications",topic:"SC-VTOL failure-condition classes — why Hazardous and Catastrophic depend on the category",text:"MOC VTOL.2510 Section 7(a) classifies failure conditions as No Safety Effect, Minor, Major, Hazardous and Catastrophic. The No Safety Effect, Minor and Major definitions follow the AMC 25.1309 / AC 23.1309-1E pattern (Minor: slight reduction in margins or capability, slight crew-workload increase, some passenger discomfort; Major: significant reduction in margins or capability, significant workload increase, physical distress possibly including injuries). Hazardous and Catastrophic are CATEGORY-DEPENDENT, and this is the SC-VTOL-specific point. For Category Enhanced, Catastrophic covers failure conditions expected to result in one or more fatalities or flight-crew incapacitation, usually with loss of the aircraft, plus any condition that would prevent continued safe flight and landing; Hazardous may include possible serious injury to an occupant other than the flight crew, but no fatality reasonably expected — fatalities are excluded from Hazardous entirely. For Category Basic, the definitions track AC 23.1309-1E: Catastrophic is multiple fatalities or flight-crew fatality/incapacitation, usually with loss of the aircraft, plus any condition that would prevent a controlled emergency landing; Hazardous includes serious OR fatal injury to an occupant other than the flight crew. The MOC's explanatory note gives the reason: Enhanced serves the air-taxi / urban-air-mobility context with high operation counts and public-safety expectations, aligning with the RPAS view that a ground fatality is Catastrophic. Fatalities counts passengers, flight crew and people on the ground. Section 7(b) gives the qualitative probability terms: Probable (anticipated once or more in each aircraft's life), Remote (unlikely per aircraft, several times across the fleet), Extremely Remote (not anticipated per aircraft, a few times across the fleet), Extremely Improbable (not anticipated across the entire fleet life)."},{id:"certstd-28",source:"EASA MOC SC-VTOL Issue 2 — MOC VTOL.2510 §6 fail-safe · §8(b) single failure and common cause · §12 latent failures",topic:"SC-VTOL single-failure, common-cause and latent-failure rules",text:"Three MOC VTOL.2510 sections carry the architecture rules an eVTOL PSSA/SSA is checked against. Section 6 (fail-safe design concept): the failure of any single element, component or connection during any one flight is to be assumed regardless of probability and must not be catastrophic; subsequent related failures in the same flight, detected or latent, and their combinations are also to be considered; and the MOC lists eleven fail-safe techniques (designed integrity and quality with life limits, redundancy or backup, isolation/segregation, proven reliability, failure warning or indication, flight-crew procedures, checkability, designed failure-effect limits, designed failure path, margins or factors of safety, and error tolerance) noting that one alone is seldom adequate. Section 8(b) (single failure and common cause): per VTOL.2510(a)(1) a catastrophic failure condition must not result from failure of a single component, part or element; failure containment must limit propagation; there must be no common-cause failure affecting both the element and its containment; a single failure includes any set of failures that cannot be shown independent; common-cause, common-mode and cascading failures are assessed as dependent failures from the root cause; errors in development, manufacturing, installation and maintenance count as common-cause sources; and protection from multiple failures is required when the first would not be detected in normal operation including pre-flight checks. Engineering judgment plus service history may show a single-failure catastrophe not a practical possibility only when the logic is straightforward and obvious. Section 12 (latent failures): periodic maintenance or crew checks are undesirable substitutes for practical, reliable monitoring; significant latent failures (those that combine with one or more specific failures or events into Hazardous or Catastrophic) are to be avoided in design; and dual-failure combinations with one latent that can lead to Catastrophic are to be avoided, highlighted in the SSA, and discussed with EASA early. Safety Lab Aero's FTA independence checks, CMA lane and latent-exposure fields are where these rules land."},{id:"certstd-29",source:"EASA MOC SC-VTOL Issue 2 — MOC VTOL.2510 §9 safety assessment process · §9(b) common mode analysis",topic:"The SC-VTOL safety-assessment process and its ARP4754A/ARP4761 anchoring",text:"MOC VTOL.2510 Section 9(a) states the safety-assessment objectives: examine aircraft and system functions to identify functional failures and classify their failure conditions; establish and validate safety requirements for aircraft, systems and items; verify that architecture and design meet those requirements and the safety objectives including the single-failure criterion; and establish and verify physical and functional separation, isolation and independence between systems and items. It names ED-79A/ARP4754A and ARP4761 as the guidance (MOC-5, in consultation from 18 July 2025, moves these to ED-79B/ARP4754B and ED-135/ARP4761A), allows an applicant to propose other guidance agreed with EASA, makes depth and scope depend on criticality and complexity, describes the process as iterative with preliminary steps and regular EASA coordination, and requires assumptions to be justified and validated. Section 9(b) is the common mode analysis: an analytical method to define independence principles and requirements and verify they were implemented, run EARLY because it shapes both the safety requirements and the architecture. It warns explicitly that IDAL A items are not immune to development error — EASA has seen an IDAL A development error take out every affected unit simultaneously — so IDAL A items stay inside CMA scope. The accepted structured approach follows ARP4761 Appendix K (K.3.1 checklists, K.3.2 independence principles, K.3.3 design analysis, K.4 documentation; MOC-5 re-points to ARP4761A Appendix M paragraphs M.3.1–M.3.3 and ED-344 for VTOL-specific CMA tailoring), with an explicit list of common-mode types: software and hardware development errors, hardware failures, production/repair flaws, stress-related events, installation errors, requirement errors, environmental factors, cascading faults and common external sources. Analysis must reach item level (AEH and software, including their architectures) and cover both availability (loss) and integrity. Fly-by-wire flight-control functions add MOC VTOL.2300. This is the process Safety Lab Aero's FHA → PSSA → SSA lanes and CMA lane instantiate for an SC-VTOL project."},{id:"certstd-30",source:"EASA MOC SC-VTOL Issue 2 — MOC VTOL.2510 §10 development assurance · §4 definitions",topic:"SC-VTOL development assurance — FDAL/IDAL allocation and the recognized AMCs",text:"MOC VTOL.2510 Section 10 sets development assurance for SC-VTOL. Section 4 defines FDAL as the development assurance level for aircraft functions, systems and equipment and IDAL for software and electronic-hardware items, both determined by the system safety assessment process. Section 10(a): initial FDAL allocation is per the Section 8(a) Table 1 ladder; ED-79A/ARP4754A Section 5.2 may be used for further FDAL/IDAL allocation, and absent agreed guidelines the IDAL of every contributing item equals the FDAL of the function. Section 10(b) recognizes ED-79A/ARP4754A as the aircraft/system development-assurance process for FDAL A–D. Section 10(c) recognizes AMC 20-115 for software, with DO-278A/ED-109A Section 12.4 as an alternative for COTS and open-source software, and gives the IDAL D alleviation for software contributing to Minor conditions: for Basic 1 and Basic 2, software-level assurance may rely on system-level processes when the equipment is a single piece developed under an acceptable process; for Basic 3 and Enhanced, only if the software high-level requirements are captured and verified in the system requirements per ARP4754A Section 5.4, with derived requirements identified, validated and verified. Section 10(d) recognizes AMC 20-152 for airborne electronic hardware and 10(e) AMC 20-189 for open-problem-report management; MOC-5 adds AMC 20-193 for multicore processors. Section 10(f) widens the ARP4754A derived requirement definition to include requirements that trace upward but add behavior not specified above. Simple systems that are not highly integrated may be treated as meeting DAL A rigour through exhaustive test, inspection and direct verification — but anything containing software or complex electronic hardware cannot be considered simple. Safety Lab Aero allocates the FDAL from Table 1 and applies DALgebra downstream; it does not run DO-178C/DO-254 V&V itself."},{id:"certstd-31",source:"EASA MOC-5 SC-VTOL Issue 1 (18 July 2025, public consultation) · MOC SC-VTOL Issue 2 §11 · MOC VTOL.2517",topic:"What the later MOC publications change — MOC-5 revisions, highly integrated lift/thrust, EWIS",text:"EASA publishes SC-VTOL means of compliance in numbered batches (MOC SC-VTOL, MOC-2, MOC-3, MOC-4, MOC-5), consolidated into the Easy Access Rules for SC-VTOL. MOC-5 SC-VTOL (Doc. No. MOC-5 SC-VTOL, Issue 1, 18 July 2025, public consultation with comments due 26 September 2025) revises MOC VTOL.2510 rather than replacing it: references move from ED-79A/ARP4754A and ARP4761 to ED-79B/ARP4754B and ED-135/ARP4761A; the development-assurance and FDAL/IDAL definitions are re-sourced to ARP4761A; the malfunction definition becomes operation different than intended, excluding loss of function; the CMA approach is re-pointed to ARP4761A Appendix M with ED-344 (common mode analysis for lift/thrust systems, Category Enhanced) for VTOL tailoring; Section 8 Note A now also grants an ED-135/ARP4761A Appendix P alleviation for additional members in Basic 1 and 2 (at least two contributing members at the Table FDAL, others not below D); AMC 25.1309 Figure A2-2 depth-of-analysis may be used with limited service-experience credit; flight-control jams handled under MOC VTOL.2300(a)(2)(d) are excepted from VTOL.2510(a)(1); AMC 20-193 covers multicore processors; and a new MOC VTOL.2517 gives EWIS guidance (qualitative, aircraft-level safety assessment of wiring, not probability-based; VTOL.2510(a)(3) Major is not applied to EWIS). MOC Issue 2 Section 11 already flags the defining eVTOL integration issue: on most VTOL designs the flight-control and lift/thrust systems are highly integrated — propulsion directly contributes to controllability — so the lift/thrust system must be developed against the Section 8 objectives under VTOL.2510, and AMC 20-170 applies where multiple functions share a system. Section 13 gives crew and maintenance credit rules: credit for correct crew action requires adequate information, time and no exceptional skill, with procedures in the AFM; scheduled maintenance may detect latent failures for hazardous/catastrophic conditions only through an agreed required task; and no MTBF credit for hidden-failure detection in double-failure cases leading to Catastrophic or Hazardous. Posture: EASA text is cited and pointed to, not reproduced; open the EASA documents for the authoritative wording."},{id:"certstd-32",source:"FAA AC 25.1309-1B (30 Aug 2024) — identity, cancellation, amendment 25-152",topic:"AC 25.1309-1B — what it is and why the paragraph numbers changed",text:'FAA Advisory Circular 25.1309-1B, "System Design and Analysis," dated 08/30/2024 and initiated by AIR-600 (signed by Daniel Elgas, Director, Policy and Standards Division, Aircraft Certification Service), describes acceptable means, but not the only means, for showing compliance with 14 CFR 25.1309, Equipment, systems, and installations. It CANCELS AC 25.1309-1A of June 21, 1988 — cite 1B, not 1A. It was issued concurrently with amendment 25-152 (89 FR 68706, August 27, 2024), which revised §25.1309 and added the Hazardous category to the rule: the FAA now addresses five failure-condition classifications (no safety effect, minor, major, hazardous, catastrophic), harmonised with EASA AMC 25.1309 (Appendix A.5). The document is organised as Chapter 1 General (1.5 Definitions), Chapter 2 Background (2.2 Fail-Safe Design Concept), Chapter 3 Failure Condition Classifications and Probability Terms (3.1 classes, 3.2 qualitative terms, 3.3 quantitative terms), Chapter 4 Safety Objective (Figure 4-1, Table 4-1), Chapter 5 Compliance with §25.1309 (5.3.4 development assurance, 5.3.5 crew and maintenance actions, 5.3.6 significant latent failures and CSL+1), Chapter 6 Identification of Failure Conditions (6.2 FHA, 6.3 assessing effects), Chapter 7 Assessment of Probabilities (7.3 single failure, 7.4 common cause, 7.5 depth of analysis, 7.6 average probability per flight hour, 7.8 operational and environmental conditions, 7.9 assumptions), Chapter 8 Operational and Maintenance Considerations (CMRs), Chapter 9 Modifications, and Appendices A (history), B (assessment methods), C (process overview, Figures C-1 and C-2), D (CSL+1 worked example), E (accepted probabilities), F (calculating average probability per flight hour), G (acronyms). Anything citing "AC 25.1309-1A paragraph 9/10/11" is the old numbering; the 1B equivalents are 3.2–3.3 (probability terms), 7.6 and Appendix F (quantitative analysis), Appendix C (process), and 7.4 / Appendix B.1.6.2 (common cause and particular risks).'},{id:"certstd-33",source:"FAA AC 25.1309-1B §3.1.1–3.1.5 · Table 4-1",topic:"Part 25 failure condition severity definitions, verbatim (AC 25.1309-1B §3.1) — the transport rubric",text:'AC 25.1309-1B Chapter 3 classifies failure conditions by the severity of their effects; the major, hazardous and catastrophic descriptions are the same as those in §25.4. No Safety Effect (3.1.1): "Failure conditions that would have no effect on safety. For example, failure conditions that would not affect the operational capability of the airplane or increase flightcrew workload but may cause inconvenience to passengers or cabin crew." Minor (3.1.2): "A failure condition that would not significantly reduce airplane safety and would only involve flightcrew actions that are well within their capabilities" — for example a slight reduction in safety margins or functional capabilities, a slight increase in flightcrew workload such as routine flight plan changes, or some physical discomfort to passengers or cabin crew. Major (3.1.3): "A failure condition that would reduce the capability of the airplane or the ability of the flightcrew to cope with adverse operating conditions, to the extent that there would be" a significant reduction in safety margins or functional capabilities, a physical discomfort or significant increase in flightcrew workload or in conditions impairing the efficiency of the flightcrew, physical distress to passengers or cabin crew possibly including injuries, or an effect of similar severity. Hazardous (3.1.4): the same reduction in capability to the extent that there would be a large reduction in safety margins or functional capabilities, physical distress or excessive workload such that the flightcrew cannot be relied upon to perform their tasks accurately or completely, or serious or fatal injuries to a relatively small number of persons other than the flightcrew. Catastrophic (3.1.5): "A failure condition that would result in multiple fatalities, usually with the loss of the airplane." Note 1: "A failure condition that would prevent continued safe flight and landing should be classified as catastrophic unless otherwise defined in other specific ACs." Note 2: "multiple fatalities" means two or more fatalities. Table 4-1 lays the same five classes out as three effect rows — effect on airplane (no effect / slight reduction in functional capabilities or safety margins / significant reduction / large reduction / normally with hull loss), effect on occupants or other persons excluding flightcrew (inconvenience / physical discomfort / physical distress possibly including injuries / serious or fatal injury to a small number of persons other than the flightcrew / multiple fatalities), and effect on flightcrew (no effect on workload / slight increase in workload / physical discomfort or significant increase in workload / physical distress or excessive workload such that flightcrew cannot be relied upon to perform their tasks accurately or completely / fatalities or incapacitation). Safety Lab Aero injects exactly these definitions into every severity-classifying pass for a Part 25 project (severity_rubrics.js).'},{id:"certstd-34",source:"FAA AC 25.1309-1B §3.2, §3.3, §4.1, Table 4-1 · Safety Lab Aero safety_targets.js",topic:'Part 25 probability terms — qualitative definitions, the quantitative ranges per flight hour, and the "on the order of" factors',text:'AC 25.1309-1B §3.2 defines the four qualitative probability terms (same as §25.4). Probable (3.2.1): "anticipated to occur one or more times during the entire operational life of each airplane of a given type." Remote (3.2.2): "not anticipated to occur to each airplane of a given type during its entire operational life, but which may occur several times during the total operational life of a number of airplanes of a given type." Extremely Remote (3.2.3): not anticipated per airplane "but which may occur a few times during the total operational life of all airplanes of a given type." Extremely Improbable (3.2.4): "not anticipated to occur during the total operational life of all airplanes of a given type." §3.3.1 gives the accepted quantitative ranges as average probability per flight hour: Probable — on the order of 1×10^-3 or less but greater than 1×10^-5; Remote — 1×10^-5 or less but greater than 1×10^-7; Extremely Remote — 1×10^-7 or less but greater than 1×10^-9; Extremely Improbable — 1×10^-9 or less. A calculated probability lower than the range is compliant. §4.1: no safety effect has no probability requirement; minor may be probable; major must be no more frequent than remote; hazardous no more frequent than extremely remote; catastrophic must be extremely improbable (Figure 4-1 draws this as the inverse relationship). Table 4-1 footnote: the minor range is a reference only — no quantitative analysis is required for minor failure conditions. "On the order of" is given a number in §3.3.1: for a remote failure condition a factor of two may be considered on the order of; for extremely remote or extremely improbable a factor of three; a higher factor may be acceptable case by case at the low end because of analysis-method uncertainty (§7.6.4). §3.3.2 and Appendix A.6: the numbers are guidelines for statistically controllable hazards, not FAA accident-rate goals, and meeting 1×10^-9 alone does not show extremely improbable if experience says the condition can occur; the values are unchanged since the Concorde-era derivation (1×10^-7 per flight hour for all systems-related catastrophes, apportioned over an assumed one hundred catastrophic conditions). Safety Lab Aero PROB_TARGETS "Part 25" carries Catastrophic 1e-9, Hazardous 1e-7, Major 1e-5, Minor 1e-3 — the top of each AC range — pinned in the regression suite.'},{id:"certstd-35",source:"FAA AC 25.1309-1B §4.3, §7.3, §3.2.4.1, §2.2",topic:"Part 25 single-failure rule, what counts as a single failure, and the fail-safe design concept",text:'AC 25.1309-1B §4.3 states the three parts of the catastrophic safety objective: no single failure results in a catastrophic failure condition; each catastrophic failure condition is extremely improbable; and significant latent failures are addressed per §25.1309(b)(4) and (b)(5). §7.3.1: per §25.1309(b)(1)(ii) "a catastrophic failure condition must not result from the failure of a single component, part, or element of a system"; the design should provide failure containment limiting propagation of any single failure, there must be no common cause failure affecting both the element and its containment, and "A single failure includes any set of failures that cannot be shown to be independent from each other" (§1.5.27 adds: e.g. failures due to a common cause). Errors in requirement specification, design, implementation, installation, crew operations, maintenance and manufacturing that could cause common-mode failures are to be assessed; Appendix B and ARP 4761 give the methods. §7.3.1 also warns that a bottom-up FMEA typically finds only dominant failure modes, and that top-down fault-tree information should focus the single-failure search on obscure modes (its example: intermittent shorting in a monitored signal path defeating monitor coverage). §7.3.2: a failure mode may be treated as not foreseeable only where any knowledgeable, experienced person would unequivocally conclude it simply would not occur unless associated with an unrelated, itself-catastrophic condition — and "Probabilistic methods may not be used in making this assessment." §3.2.4.1.2: the FAA does not consider a single-failure condition extremely improbable unless the operational or environmental conditions needed are themselves extremely remote or the physics is implausible; catastrophic showings therefore normally involve multiple failures, and quantitative analysis alone is generally insufficient. §2.2 fail-safe concept: assume the failure of any single element during any one flight regardless of probability, consider subsequent failures in the same flight (detected or latent), and combine the eleven techniques — designed integrity and quality with life limits, redundancy or backup, isolation/segregation, proven reliability, failure annunciation or indication, flightcrew procedures, checkability, designed failure effect limits, designed failure path, margins or factors of safety, and error tolerance.'},{id:"certstd-36",source:"FAA AC 25.1309-1B §1.5.5, §1.5.15, §1.5.21, §1.5.26, §5.3.6, Appendix D",topic:"Part 25 significant latent failures, the 1/1000 latency limit, CSL+1 and the residual-risk criterion",text:`AC 25.1309-1B carries the amendment 25-152 latent-failure rules. Definitions: Latent Failure (1.5.21) "A failure that is not detected or annunciated when it occurs." Exposure Time (1.5.15) "The time between when an item is known to be operating properly and when it will be known to be operating properly again." Significant Latent Failure, SLF (1.5.26, also §25.4): "A latent failure that, in combination with one or more specific failures or events, would result in a hazardous or catastrophic failure condition." CSL+1 (1.5.5): "A catastrophic failure condition that results from a combination of two failures, either of which could be latent for more than one flight." §5.3.6.2: the (b)(4) elimination/minimisation requirement does not apply where the failure condition still meets (b)(1) or (b)(2) with the latent failure assumed present. §5.3.6.3 hierarchy: first eliminate SLFs to the maximum practical extent using state-of-the-art monitoring and crew indication; then, for each SLF that cannot reasonably be eliminated, minimise exposure time by design (pilot-initiated or self-initiated checks such as first-flight-of-day or power-up built-in tests) rather than lengthy scheduled-maintenance intervals. §5.3.6.4 acceptable means: limit latency so that "the product of this exposure time and the average failure rate of the SLF does not exceed 1/1000," or show the failure would not be latent for more than one flight. §5.3.6.5: where 1/1000 is impractical (e.g. invasive line maintenance), minimise the time the failure is expected to be present. §5.3.6.7–5.3.6.9 for CSL+1: first show additional fault tolerance (monitors, redundancy) is impractical with rationale, then apply §25.1309(b)(5)(ii) residual risk and (iii) limit latency — the worked balance: with latency at 1/1000 the residual risk must be on the order of 1×10^-6 per flight hour or better to meet 1×10^-9; conversely with a residual component at 1×10^-5 per flight hour, latency is limited to 1×10^-4. Appendix D method on the fault tree's minimal cutsets: group dual-order CSL+1 cutsets by common latent event and sum the active failures — on the order of 1×10^-5 per flight hour or less (residual risk); group by common active event and sum the latent probabilities — not to exceed 1/1000 (limit latency); and the sum of all cutsets on the order of 1×10^-9 or less for (b)(1). P[latent] ≈ failure rate × exposure time. §5.4.6 and §8.3.1: periodic maintenance or crew checks to detect SLFs are undesirable in place of practical monitoring; where used they become candidate CMRs handled per AC 25-19A and placed in the Airworthiness Limitations Section under §25.1309(e). Safety Lab Aero's FTA latency and CSL+1 checks implement these numbers.`},{id:"certstd-37",source:"FAA AC 25.1309-1B §7.5, §1.5.7, §1.5.8, Appendix C Figure C-1",topic:"Part 25 depth of analysis — simple, conventional, complex, and what each severity class requires",text:`AC 25.1309-1B §7.2.3: identifying and classifying failure conditions is necessarily qualitative; assessing their probability may be qualitative or quantitative, with depth depending on the functions, the severity, and whether the system is complex. Definitions: Complex (1.5.7) "A system is complex when its operation, failure modes, or failure effects are difficult to comprehend without the aid of analytical methods." Conventional (1.5.8) "A system is conventional if its functionality, the technological means used to implement its functionality, and its intended usage are all the same as, or closely similar to, that of previously approved systems that are commonly used." §7.5 by class: No Safety Effect (7.5.1) and Minor (7.5.2) — an FHA with a design and installation appraisal establishing independence from other functions; the applicant may derive the safety effects from the appraisal alone for a simple function, documented; where complexity or integration defeats an appraisal, ARP 4754/4761 methods apply. Major (7.5.3) — must be remote; similarity to previously used systems plus satisfactory service history is usually acceptable if differences are identified and the applicability of the history justified (7.5.3.1); non-complex systems without similarity use a qualitative assessment consistent with the FHA (7.5.3.2); complex systems without redundancy may need a qualitative functional FMEA supported by failure-rate data and fault-detection coverage analysis (7.5.3.3); for complex redundant systems a qualitative FMEA and qualitative fault tree may be needed to show redundancy actually exists (7.5.3.4). Hazardous and Catastrophic (7.5.4) — a detailed safety analysis, usually a combination of qualitative and quantitative assessment, for each such condition the FHA identifies (7.5.4.1); the qualitative-only exceptions are very simple and conventional installations judged on redundancy, established independence, channel isolation and the technology's reliability record (7.5.4.2), and complex systems where true similarity in all relevant attributes including installation is rigorously established and the applicant can show the baseline complies, which typically requires access to all the baseline type design data (7.5.4.3). Figure C-1, the Depth of Analysis Flowchart, encodes the same decisions: conduct the FHA; is there a safety effect; is it minor; is it major; is the system similar to a previous design; simple and conventional; redundant; simple — leading to design and installation appraisal, verify similarity, qualitative assessment, or qualitative plus quantitative assessment. §7.7.3: software and hardware complexity need not dominate system-level complexity — a predictive windshear system may be complex to build yet have failures that summarise as false alerts, misleading information and loss of prediction.`},{id:"certstd-38",source:"FAA AC 25.1309-1B §1.5.4, §7.6, Appendix F",topic:"Part 25 average probability per flight hour — definition, at-risk time, per-flight conversion, and the four-step Appendix F calculation",text:`AC 25.1309-1B §1.5.4 defines Average Probability per Flight Hour as "the quotient of the number of times the subject failure condition is predicted to occur during the entire operating life of all airplanes of the type divided by the anticipated total operating hours of all airplanes of that type," normally calculated as the probability of the failure condition during a typical flight of mean duration divided by that mean duration. §7.6.1 requires the calculation to consider the average flight duration and profile for the type (ETOPS per K25.1 may change this), all combinations of failures and events contributing, the conditional probability where a sequence is needed, the relevant at-risk time (1.5.2: the period during which the aircraft may be subject to the failure effect) — a condition relevant only in certain phases must meet the criteria under those conditions, applied as a probability per flight and converted to per flight hour by dividing by ONE hour, not by the mean flight duration — and the total exposure time if a failure can persist for multiple flights. §7.6.3: if the per-mean-flight estimate would differ significantly from the fleet-life rate (e.g. consumable loss on long flights), a better model of the flight of average risk must be used. §7.6.4: component failure-rate data are not precise; account for the uncertainty conservatively. Appendix F four steps: (1) determine the average flight — duration as cumulative flight hours over cumulative flights for the service life, profile in ISA at average weight, phase durations from the profile, taxi added where appropriate; (2) calculate the probability of the failure condition for that average flight by structured methods, using mature constant failure rates after infant mortality and before wear-out, all causes, service history where available, with latent elements carrying their exposure intervals and phase-dependent rates integrated phase by phase (F.3.3, checked-operative-at-start versus unknown-state cases), and sequence-dependent conditional probabilities (F.3.4); (3) calculate the average probability per flight over the N flights of the relevant period (least common multiple of exposure times or airplane life) by summing per-flight probabilities and dividing by N (F.4); (4) divide by the average flight duration T_F in hours to obtain the average probability per flight hour and compare it with the class objective from the FHA (F.5). F.3.1.1–F.3.1.2: aging and wear of similarly constructed redundant components that could lead to hazardous or catastrophic conditions must be assessed for scheduled maintenance and documented in the ALS. This is the arithmetic behind Safety Lab Aero's phase-exposure normalisation and mission-duration fields.`},{id:"certstd-39",source:"FAA AC 25.1309-1B §7.8, Appendix E Tables E-1 to E-5",topic:"Part 25 accepted probabilities for environmental and operational conditions (Appendix E) — usable without further justification",text:'AC 25.1309-1B §7.8.1: a probability of 1 is normally used for a discrete condition the airplane is designed for (IMC, Category III operations, AFM landing field length), but Appendix E lists allowable probabilities for operational and environmental conditions that may be used in computing the average probability per flight hour WITHOUT further justification; where a table says "No accepted standard data" the applicant must justify any value below 1; the condition and the system failure must be independent or the dependency accounted for. §7.8.2: single failures in combination with operational or environmental conditions leading to catastrophic conditions are generally not acceptable, but single failures need not be assumed in combination with conditions that are extremely remote or outside the normal flight envelope of AC 25.671-1; a non-redundant protection system against such a condition (e.g. cargo fire detection and suppression) should meet the major failure condition criteria for any single failure that loses the protection. Table E-1 environmental: Appendix C icing, probability 1; Appendix O supercooled large drop icing, 10^-2 per flight hour (AC 25-28); head wind greater than 25 knots at takeoff/landing, 10^-2 per flight (AC 120-28D / CS-AWO) or 5×10^-3 per flight (NLR-CR-2016-601); tail wind greater than 10 knots, 10^-2 or 3×10^-3 per flight; cross wind greater than 20 knots, 10^-2 or 3×10^-3 per flight; limit design gust and turbulence, 10^-5 per flight hour (§25.341); air temperature below -70 °C, no accepted standard data. Table E-2 configurations: centre of gravity and takeoff/landing weights, 1 (uniform over the approved range). Table E-3 flight conditions: condition requiring stall warning, 10^-2 per flight (in-service) or 4×10^-6 per flight / 2.5×10^-6 per flight hour (NLR); a stall, 10^-5 per flight or 5×10^-8 per flight / 3×10^-8 per flight hour; VMO/MMO exceedance, 10^-2 per flight or 2×10^-3 per flight / 3×10^-4 per flight hour; load factor at or above 1.5 g due to gusts, 7×10^-3 per flight; at or below 0 g, 1×10^-6 per flight / 4×10^-7 per flight hour. Table E-4 mission: any rejected takeoff 1.5×10^-4 per flight; high-energy (near V1) rejected takeoff 7×10^-6 per flight; need to jettison fuel 1.5×10^-4 per flight / 2.5×10^-4 per flight hour; go-around 7×10^-4 per flight (within the normal envelope). Table E-5 other events: fire in a lavatory 2.5×10^-7 per flight / 1.5×10^-7 per flight hour; fire in a cargo compartment 4×10^-8 per flight / 3.5×10^-8 per flight hour. These are the numbers a Part 25 fault tree may attach to an environmental or operational basic event; the AC may not be appropriate for other regulations.'},{id:"certstd-40",source:"FAA AC 25.1309-1B §6.2, §6.3, §5.4.2, §6.1",topic:"Part 25 FHA requirements and the factors that decide a severity — intensifying, alleviating, annunciation loss, combinations",text:`AC 25.1309-1B §6.2.1: before detailed assessment, "An FHA is a systematic, comprehensive examination of airplane and system functions to identify potential no safety effect, minor, major, hazardous, and catastrophic failure conditions that may arise as a result of malfunctions or failures to function as intended," performed early and updated as the design develops (6.2.3), considering operational vulnerabilities rather than implementation, including normal responses to unusual external factors, and used to set the safety objectives and the function development assurance levels. §6.2.2: examine each function against the other functions of the same system and against related functions of other systems, because combined loss can be more severe. §6.2.4: separate system FHAs are feasible where interfaces are few and simple; complex or integrated designs take a top-down airplane-level approach with common-cause considerations. §6.1 identifies failure conditions at three levels — airplane functions (independent of implementation), system functions (via FMEA-type methods, with the note that combinations found through FHA, PRA, ZSA and CMA may be more severe than individual conditions: minor or major individually can be hazardous or catastrophic in combination), and equipment intrinsic hazards (energy storage, chemicals, pressure bottles, thermal runaway). §6.3.2 evaluates severity by effects on the airplane, on the flightcrew (workload, human-factors assumptions about detection and response), on occupants, and on persons other than occupants such as ground crew. §6.3.3.2: classification always considers all relevant internal and external factors; "Where flight duration, flight phase, or maximum length diversion time can adversely affect the FHA outcome, they must be considered as intensifying factors"; weather and adverse conditions reducing airplane or crew capability intensify; alleviating factors are continued performance of similar functions by unaffected systems and crew recognition and action, which may temper effects "but it should not be used in lieu of appropriate system design, integrity, and availability requirements" — and the case where the crew does not act or acts late must also be considered, which may make the condition an unsafe system operating condition under §25.1309(c). §6.3.3.3: for a hazardous condition show remaining capability prevents it becoming catastrophic. §5.4.2: loss of annunciation is a failure condition in itself, "classified as major unless the applicant can show otherwise," and the combination of a system failure with failure of its annunciation must meet the objective of the un-annunciated effect. These are the exact rules Safety Lab Aero's severity rubric (severity_rubrics.js) puts in front of every FHA, FCIM, FMEA and review pass on a Part 25 project.`},{id:"certstd-41",source:"FAA AC 25.1309-1B §5.3.3, §5.3.4, §7.4, §7.7, Appendix B, Appendix C",topic:"Part 25 process — ARP 4754/4761 recognition, development assurance, common cause sources, and the assessment methods the AC names",text:"AC 25.1309-1B §5.3.3 recognizes SAE ARP 4754 and ARP 4761 methods, when correctly applied, as valid for showing compliance with §25.1309(b), without constraining the applicant to them; §7.7.2 calls ARP 4754 the industry standard of practice for highly integrated or complex systems. §5.3.4: errors in requirements, design and implementation must be considered; direct exhaustive test and inspection may still suit simple, non-integrated systems, while integrated or complex systems use development assurance with rigour set by the severity of the effects — acceptable guidelines are AC 20-174 (ARP 4754 for aircraft and systems), AC 20-115D (DO-178 software, supplements and tool qualification) and AC 20-152A (DO-254 airborne electronic hardware); development assurance must define and validate all requirements including derived safety requirements and verify fault containment so integrated systems are fail-safe. §7.4 lists common-cause sources to consider with cascading effects: rapid energy release from uncontained rotating parts (other than engines and propellers) or pressure vessels; pressure differentials; non-catastrophic structural failures; loss of environmental conditioning; disconnection of more than one subsystem by overtemperature devices; fluid contamination; localized fires; loss of power supply or return; failure of sensors feeding multiple systems; excessive voltage; physical or environmental interactions among parts; requirements, design, implementation, installation, operations, maintenance and manufacturing errors; and events external to the system or airplane. Appendix B assessment methods: design appraisal and installation appraisal (qualitative); FMEA — structured, inductive, bottom-up, piece-part or functional, with a functional FMEA's uncertainty compensated by conservative assumptions, architecture choice or lessons learned (B.1.3); fault tree or dependence diagram — structured, deductive, top-down, with the FMEA as source of primary events (B.1.4); Markov analysis, recommended where fault trees cannot represent complex transition states (B.1.5); and the three independence studies (B.1.6) — Zonal Safety Analysis for installation standards, interference and maintenance error within each zone, Particular Risk Analysis for events outside the systems concerned (fire, leaking fluids, bird strike, tire burst, HIRF, lightning, uncontained high-energy rotating machines), and Common Mode Analysis to confirm the assumed independence of combined events considering specification, design, implementation, installation, maintenance and manufacturing errors, environmental factors and component failures. Appendix C.2.8 presents the process as a V: airplane FHA and system FHAs on the left, analyses (FTA, FMEA, ZSA, PRA, Cascading Effects Analysis, CMA), PSSA then SSA per system, and the Aircraft Safety Assessment (ASA) integrating the SSAs — the requirement that systems be evaluated in relation to other systems. C.2.5 lists what a compliant analysis documents: functions, boundaries and interfaces; parts and equipment with development assurance levels; failure conditions with classifications and probabilities; and a traceable basis for each classification with common-cause precautions, failure-rate sources, assumptions and required crew or CCMR actions. This is the lane structure Safety Lab Aero implements: FHA → PSSA → SSA → ASA with ZSA/PRA/CMA/CEA as the independence evidence."},{id:"certstd-42",source:"FAA AC 29-2C Chg 4 (5/1/2014) — AC 29.1309 a., b.(1)",topic:"AC 29-2C §29.1309 — applicability of the transport-rotorcraft system-safety rule and the five probability terms with ranges",text:`FAA AC 29-2C, "Certification of Transport Category Rotorcraft," carries its §29.1309 guidance in section AC 29.1309 (Amendment 29-40; Change 4, 5/1/2014, pages F-13 onward) and AC 29.1309A (Amendment 29-53, which moved lightning to the dedicated §29.1316 and otherwise leaves the section applicable). Applicability (a.(1)): §29.1309 is a general requirement for any equipment or system as installed, in addition to specific systems requirements; a specific Part 29 rule that predefines redundancy or criticality (e.g. §29.695) takes precedence but does not preclude a safety assessment; it does not apply to Subparts B, C and D performance, flight-characteristics, loads and strength requirements, but does apply to equipment those requirements rely on (e.g. HUMS with maintenance credit, stability augmentation); it does not apply to the uninstalled type-certificated engine (installation equipment yes, §29.901) nor to the rotor drive system (§29.917(b)); and it does not apply to the functional aspects of stowed safety equipment, non-safety equipment such as entertainment systems, hoists, FLIR or medical equipment, or the lighting characteristics of position and anti-collision lights — while still applying to hazards those installations introduce (EMI, fire, inadvertent flotation deployment, glare). b.(1) probability classifications, per flight hour: FREQUENT — on the order of 10^-3 or greater (the lower part of the old "probable" range); REASONABLY PROBABLE — between 10^-3 and 10^-5; REMOTE — between 10^-5 and 10^-7, expected a few times in the life of a single rotorcraft of the type but several times across the fleet; EXTREMELY REMOTE — between 10^-7 and 10^-9, not expected in a single rotorcraft's life but a few times across all rotorcraft of the type; EXTREMELY IMPROBABLE — 10^-9 or less, so unlikely they need not be considered to ever occur unless engineering judgment requires it. The terms relate to failure conditions, not to the reliability of specific components.`},{id:"certstd-43",source:"FAA AC 29-2C Chg 4 — AC 29.1309 b.(2), b.(3), Figure AC 29.1309-1/-2/-3 · Safety Lab Aero safety_targets.js",topic:"Part 29 failure condition classes verbatim, the safety objective, and Figure AC 29.1309-2 (the numbers the engine carries)",text:'AC 29.1309 b.(2) classifies failure conditions by severity. No Effect: "Failure Conditions that would have no effect on safety; for example, Failure Conditions that would not affect the operational capability of the rotorcraft or increase crew workload, however, could result in an inconvenience to the occupants, excluding the flight crew." Minor: "Failure conditions which would not significantly reduce rotorcraft safety, and which would involve crew actions that are well within their capabilities" — slight reduction in margins or capability, slight workload increase such as routine flight plan changes, some physical discomfort to occupants. Major: "Failure conditions which would reduce the capability of the rotorcraft or the ability of the crew to cope with adverse operating conditions to the extent that there would be, for example, a significant reduction in safety margins or functional capabilities, a significant increase in crew work load or in conditions impairing crew efficiency, physical distress to occupants, possibly including injuries, or physical discomfort to the flight crew." Hazardous or Severe-Major: the same reduction to the extent of (A) a large reduction in safety margins or functional capabilities, (B) physical distress or excessive workload such that the flight crew could not be relied on to perform their tasks accurately or completely, or (C) possible serious or fatal injury to a passenger or a cabin crew member, excluding the flight crew — with the note that such conditions can include events manageable by crew procedure which, if not implemented correctly or in time, may become Catastrophic. Catastrophic: "Failure Conditions which would result in multiple fatalities to occupants, fatalities or incapacitation to the flight crew, or result in loss of rotorcraft." b.(3) Safety Objective: an inverse relationship between average probability per flight hour and severity (Figure AC 29.1309-1) — No Effect may be more frequent than Reasonably Probable; Minor may be Reasonably Probable; Major no more frequent than Remote; Hazardous no more frequent than Extremely Remote; Catastrophic must be Extremely Improbable; and for Catastrophic, additionally, no single failure will result in a Catastrophic failure condition. Figure AC 29.1309-2 tabulates it: No Effect — no probability requirement, DO-178C level E; Minor — ≤10^-3 (Note 1: reference only, no quantitative analysis required for minor), level D; Major — ≤10^-5, level C; Hazardous or Severe-Major — ≤10^-7, level B; Catastrophic — ≤10^-9, level A. Note 2: the "serious or fatal injury to a passenger or cabin crew member" Hazardous cell holds only if the condition can be contained to a fatal injury of ONE occupant. Note 3: the software levels are a correlation to the category, not a probability. Figure AC 29.1309-3 maps the older three-category rule language (non-essential / essential / critical; probable > 10^-5, improbable < 10^-5, extremely improbable < 10^-9) onto the five categories. Safety Lab Aero PROB_TARGETS "Part 29" = Catastrophic 1e-9, Hazardous 1e-7, Major 1e-5, Minor 1e-3 with DAL A/B/C/D — verified cell for cell against the fetched AC on 31 Aug 2026 and pinned.'},{id:"certstd-44",source:"FAA AC 29-2C Chg 4 — AC 29.1309 b.(4)–(9), Figures AC 29.1309-4/-5",topic:"Part 29 safety assessment process, depth of analysis, methods, CMRs, software/AEH and environmental qualification",text:'AC 29.1309 b.(4) lays out the safety assessment process (ARP 4761 for the methods, ARP 4754A via AC 20-174 for how it ties to development): define the system, interfaces and functions and decide whether it is complex, similar or conventional; identify and classify failure conditions through a top-down FHA that starts at the rotorcraft operational level, considers loss, erroneous or misleading and degraded function, evaluates against the intended operations and their combinations (VFR, IFR, Category A, IFR plus Category A), and yields a category per system function — with the warning that too many FHA levels make the assessment design-dependent instead of the FHA driving the design; base the FHA on design/installation/service-experience appraisal for non-complex similar systems or on systematic postulation of individual and combined failures for complex ones (complex: operation, failure modes or effects difficult to comprehend without analytical methods); choose the means — for Major conditions, experienced judgment, appraisals and similarity may suffice alone or with qualitative or selective quantitative analysis, while Catastrophic and Hazardous need a thorough assessment (Figure AC 29.1309-5, the depth-of-analysis flowchart, notes they will likely require both qualitative and quantitative analysis depending on complexity); document functions, boundaries and interfaces, parts and development assurance levels, conclusions with classifications and probabilities, and a traceable basis including common-cause precautions, failure-rate sources, assumptions and required crew or ground-crew actions including CCMRs (also called CCRs — CMRs are not the preferred means and need FAA approval). b.(4)(F): CMRs may be needed for significant latent failures, with intervals from quantitative analysis or service data and reasonable tolerances; failure-rate imprecision is why the ranges say "on the order of". b.(4)(I): the numerical range is the allowable risk per flight hour on a flight of mean duration, but a function used only at a specific time is assessed for that period as a per-flight-condition risk, and a system that can fail undetected must include the latent exposure time; credit for an operating condition that does not always exist is normally not allowed without FAA approval. b.(5) assessment methods: design appraisal, installation appraisal, FMEA (piece-part or functional; conservative compensation for functional FMEAs; failure-rate sources such as MIL-HDBK-217C, operator defect records, life tests), fault tree or dependence diagram, Markov analysis, and Common Cause Analysis split into Zonal Safety Analysis, Particular Risk Analysis (fire, leaking fluids, bird strike, tire burst, HIRF, lightning, uncontained high-energy rotating machines) and Common Mode Analysis (including design dissimilarity for hardware and software and the determination of which fault-tree inputs must be independent for Catastrophic or Hazardous events). b.(7)–(8): DO-178C via AC 20-115C for software; DO-254 for complex custom micro-coded AEH at levels A, B or C, encouraged up to LRU and system level. b.(9): DO-160 environmental qualification with the installed-temperature margin method (3.6 °F per 1,000 ft lapse, §29.1043(b) 100 °F minimum).'},{id:"certstd-45",source:"FAA AC 27-1B Chg 4 (3/31/2014) — AC 27.1309 c., d., f.(1)",topic:"AC 27-1B §27.1309 — failure analyses, the safety assessment, the five probability terms, and the Part 27 criticality categories verbatim",text:`FAA AC 27-1B, "Certification of Normal Category Rotorcraft," section AC 27.1309 (Amendment 27-21; Change 4, 3/31/2014, pages F-13 onward; AC 27.1309A for Amendment 27-46 moves lightning to §27.1316). c. Failure Analyses: power and distribution systems are analysed per SAE ARP 926B; the analysis considers the effect of failures on the rotorcraft's ability to perform its intended function without hazard, the indication of failure, latent failures in all sequences and combinations until a positive indication exists, and indirectly related failures (hydraulic fluid on electrical components, a ruptured bleed-air line on hydraulic, fuel or electrical lines); the TIA calls for simulated failures on the ground and in flight. d. Safety Assessment: from a simple analysis showing no catastrophic, hazardous or major conditions up to an FHA with directed analyses; ARP 4754A is endorsed for function and design assurance and ARP 4761 as the guide for the analyses; the FHA is top-down from flight operations, identifies the criticality level of each function's failure or malfunction using the five levels of paragraph f., and directs bottom-up (reliability) analysis at the function's source. d.(2)(ii)(B) defines five probability classifications, per flight hour of a flight of mean duration (a function used only at a specific time is assessed for that period): FREQUENT — on the order of 10^-3 or greater; REASONABLY PROBABLE — between 10^-3 and 10^-5; REMOTE — between 10^-5 and 10^-7; EXTREMELY REMOTE — between 10^-7 and 10^-9; EXTREMELY IMPROBABLE — 10^-9 or less. The ranges are considered to overlap because probability estimates are inexact; failure-rate sources are MIL-HDBK-217, operator or manufacturer defect records, and laboratory life tests. Note: "Generally, the guidance for Failure Analysis of paragraph c. above, is not required in its entirety for non-IFR rated rotorcraft. The only failure or reliability requirement is that no single failure can result in a hazard to the rotorcraft." f.(1) criticality categories, verbatim: Catastrophic — "failure conditions that would prevent continued safe flight and landing"; Hazardous or Severe-Major — reduce capability to the extent of "(A) A large reduction in safety margins or functional capabilities. (B) Physical distress or higher workload such that the flight crew could not be relied on to perform their tasks accurately or completely. (C) Adverse effects on occupants, including serious or potentially fatal injuries, to a small number of those occupants."; Major — significant reduction in margins or capability, significant increase in crew workload or conditions impairing crew efficiency, or discomfort to occupants possibly including injuries; Minor — "failure conditions that would not significantly reduce aircraft safety, and would involve crew actions that are well within their capabilities"; No Effect — "failure conditions that do not affect the operational capability of the aircraft or increase crew workload." Note what AC 27-1B does NOT contain: unlike AC 29-2C Figure AC 29.1309-2, it tabulates no per-severity probability objective; f.(2) ties DO-178C levels to the categories only loosely (Level A typically for flight controls at Catastrophic, B for hazardous, C for major, D and E below) with architecture, dissimilar redundancy and monitors as moderating factors, and f.(3) says the software levels have no correlation with probability.`},{id:"certstd-46",source:'FAA policy PS-ASW-27-15 "Safety Continuum for Part 27 Normal Category Rotorcraft Systems and Equipment" (30 June 2017) · 2017 FAA/EASA symposium draft table · Federal Register 2023-02771 · Safety Lab Aero safety_targets.js',topic:"The Part 27 safety continuum — four classes, the objective grid, and what is and is not verified",text:"The per-class objectives for Part 27 come from FAA policy statement PS-ASW-27-15, Safety Continuum for Part 27 Normal Category Rotorcraft Systems and Equipment, dated June 30, 2017, which the FAA reconfirmed in the 2023 rotorcraft certification rule (Federal Register 2023-02771, 10 Feb 2023) as an optional tiered approach to §27.1309 based upon the rotorcraft's weight, occupant capacity, and number and type of engines. Its four classes, as presented at the 2017 FAA/EASA safety-continuum symposium: Class I — reciprocating engine, five or fewer occupants including crew; Class II — single turbine engine, five or fewer occupants, up to 4,000 lb maximum gross weight; Class III — single turbine engine, six or more occupants, 4,001 to 7,000 lb; Class IV — twin turbine. The symposium (draft) objective grid, per flight hour with DAL: Class I — Minor <10^-3 D, Major <10^-4 C, Hazardous <10^-5 C, Catastrophic <10^-6 C; Class II — 10^-3 D, 10^-5 C, 10^-6 C, 10^-7 C; Class III — 10^-3 D, 10^-5 C, 10^-7 C, 10^-8 B; Class IV — 10^-3 D, 10^-5 C, 10^-7 B, 10^-9 A. That is the same grid as AC 23.1309-1E Figure 2 Classes I–IV. VERIFICATION STATUS (updated 1 Sep 2026): the FINAL FAA policy PDF sits behind the FAA DRS / GAMA member login and could not be fetched, so the FAA class DEFINITIONS above are still those of the 2017 draft; the NUMBERS, however, are now verified — EASA AMC1 27.1309 Table 2 (CS-27 Amendment 10, ED Decision 2023/001/R) publishes exactly this grid for its own four classes (see the CS-27 chunk). Safety Lab Aero carries the grid in PROB_TARGETS/DAL_TARGETS Part 27 I through Part 27 IV, verified against EASA and pinned; only the FAA class thresholds keep a caveat in the AC 1309 tab, the spine citation and this corpus; the legacy single Part 27 row is an alias of Class III (stricter than or equal to the previous row in every cell). Retraction: before 31 Aug 2026 the tool carried one Part 27 row (Cat 1e-7, Haz 1e-6, Maj 1e-4, DAL B/C/C/D) that matched no class. When the final policy text is obtained, replace the grid and remove the caveat."},{id:"certstd-47",source:'FAA draft policy PS-AIR-21.17-01 "Safety Continuum for Powered-lift" (regulations.gov FAA-2024-1656) · FAA AC 27-1B / AC 29-2C',topic:"Powered-lift under the FAA — the draft continuum levels and how they relate to SC-VTOL and Part 27/29",text:`For powered-lift certified under the FAA special-class route (§21.17(b)), the FAA published for comment a draft policy PS-AIR-21.17-01, "Safety Continuum for Powered-lift," in the docket FAA-2024-1656. It defines certification levels by maximum passenger seating and weight, split by whether passengers are carried for compensation or hire: 0–1 passengers and ≤12,500 lb → level 1A (with) / 1B (without); 2–6 passengers ≤12,500 lb → 2A / 2B; 7–9 passengers ≤12,500 lb → 3A / 3B; 10–19 passengers or >12,500 lb → 4A / 4B. Draft safety objectives per flight hour with FDAL: level 1B — probable <10^-3 D, remote <10^-4 D, extremely remote <10^-5 C, extremely improbable <10^-6 C; levels 1A and 2B — 10^-3 D, 10^-4 D, 10^-6 C, 10^-7 B; levels 2A and 3B — 10^-3 D, 10^-5 C, 10^-7 C, 10^-8 B; levels 3A, 4A and 4B — 10^-3 D, 10^-5 C, 10^-7 B, 10^-9 A. The draft cites PS-ASW-27-15 as the model for tiering. Read against EASA MOC SC-VTOL Table 1: the FAA seat bands (0–1 / 2–6 / 7–9) are the SC-VTOL Basic 1/2/3 bands, and the commercial-passenger column (A) is the analogue of Category Enhanced. Posture: DRAFT policy — not a certification basis in Safety Lab Aero; cite it as context for an eVTOL program choosing between the EASA SC-VTOL basis and an FAA special class, and never as the engine's numbers.`},{id:"certstd-48",source:"14 CFR §33.75 (Amdt. 33-24, 72 FR 50867, Sept. 4, 2007) · FAA AC 33.75-1A Chg 1 (2/20/15) ¶6, ¶8, ¶9",topic:"§33.75 engine safety analysis — the engine-level effect classes, the two probability criteria, and why aircraft-level classes do not apply directly",text:`14 CFR §33.75, "Safety analysis," is the engine-level counterpart of §25.1309, and it deliberately does NOT use the aircraft five-class ladder. AC 33.75-1A ¶6.a: "Section 33.75 defines the engine-level effects and presumed severity levels. The severity classifications of aircraft-level failure effects do not apply directly to engine safety assessments because the aircraft may have features that could reduce or increase the consequences of an engine failure condition," and the same type-certificated engine may be installed in several aircraft with different aircraft-level classifications of the same effect; ¶6.b: the installer's requirements may be more severe, so engine and aircraft manufacturers coordinate. §33.75(a)(1): analyse the engine including the control system for the likely consequences of all failures that can reasonably be expected to occur, accounting for aircraft-level devices and procedures assumed for a typical installation (assumptions stated, and per AC ¶7 carried into the §33.5(c) installation instructions), consequential secondary and latent failures, and multiple failures. (a)(2): summarise the failures that could result in major or hazardous engine effects with estimated probabilities, and clearly identify every engine part whose failure could reasonably result in a hazardous engine effect. (a)(3), the hazardous criterion, verbatim: "hazardous engine effects are predicted to occur at a rate not in excess of that defined as extremely remote (probability range of 10−7 to 10−9 per engine flight hour)" — and, because individual failure probabilities may be too imprecise to sum, "compliance may be shown by demonstrating that the probability of a hazardous engine effect arising from an individual failure can be predicted to be not greater than 10−8 per engine flight hour"; absolute proof is not possible at this order of magnitude, so engineering judgment and previous experience combined with sound design and test philosophies are relied on. AC ¶8.c(1) makes the two routes explicit: for EACH hazardous engine effect, either every individual way of causing it is below 10^-8 per engine flight hour, or all ways combined (excluding critical-part primary failures) sum to below 10^-7. (a)(4), the major criterion: "major engine effects are predicted to occur at a rate not in excess of that defined as remote (probability range of 10−5 to 10−7 per engine flight hour)" — AC ¶9.b: shown by individual failures or combinations each not greater than 10^-5, with no summation required. Safety Lab Aero's PROB_TARGETS "Part 33" row carries Hazardous 1e-7 (the per-effect summed criterion §33.75(a)(3) sets, symmetric with Part 35), Major 1e-5, Catastrophic 1e-9 and Minor 1e-3; the last two are aircraft-level extensions §33.75 does not define, kept so an engine-installation FHA can classify aircraft-level consequences. The ≤1e-8 individual-failure alternative-compliance route lives in the severity rubric (§33.75(g)), not this scalar. (Ruling 1 Sep 2026: the row previously carried the 1e-8 individual-failure figure; corrected to 1e-7 so the engine top-event budget is symmetric with Part 35 and matches §33.75(a)(3).)`},{id:"certstd-49",source:"14 CFR §33.75(g) · FAA AC 33.75-1A ¶18–¶20",topic:"Minor, major and hazardous ENGINE effects — the verbatim definitions and the AC's scope lists",text:`§33.75(g) supplies the failure definitions that apply to the engine unless the FAA approves otherwise. Minor engine effect, (g)(1) verbatim: "An engine failure in which the only consequence is partial or complete loss of thrust or power (and associated engine services) from the engine will be regarded as a minor engine effect." AC ¶18.c: loss of thrust is expected in service and the aircraft is assumed capable of controlled flight after it — a comparatively safe failure at engine level; failure to achieve a certificated rating is also minor; the installer may revisit both (notably for multi-engine rotorcraft) without affecting engine certification. Hazardous engine effects, (g)(2) verbatim: "(i) Non-containment of high-energy debris; (ii) Concentration of toxic products in the engine bleed air intended for the cabin sufficient to incapacitate crew or passengers; (iii) Significant thrust in the opposite direction to that commanded by the pilot; (iv) Uncontrolled fire; (v) Failure of the engine mount system leading to inadvertent engine separation; (vi) Release of the propeller by the engine, if applicable; and (vii) Complete inability to shut the engine down." AC ¶19 scope: disks, hubs, impellers, large rotating seals and similar large rotating components ALWAYS represent high-energy debris; a single released blade is designed to be contained; multiple-blade releases are typically low-energy (major) unless many blades go (corn-cobbed rotor); fan blades may retain significant energy and need case-by-case judgement; rupture of the highest-pressure (compressor-delivery) casing is hazardous. Toxic bleed air is hazardous when it is too quick-acting to stop, cannot be stopped, or is undetectable before incapacitation — no cabin dilution assumptions at engine level, and delivery rates go to the installer. Reverse thrust: uncommanded reverser deployment, propeller blades moving below the minimum in-flight low-pitch position, high forward thrust when reverse is commanded. Uncontrolled fire: extensive or persistent, not confined to a fire zone or not extinguishable by the assumed aircraft means. Inability to shut down: a delay of several minutes, normally not more than 5, between shutdown initiation and end of combustion is acceptable, and aircraft-supplied fuel cutoff may be credited. Major engine effect, (g)(3): an effect between (g)(1) and (g)(2); AC ¶20.b guide list: controlled fires, case burnthrough with no propagation to hazardous, low-energy debris release, vibration causing crew discomfort, toxic bleed concentrations that degrade but do not incapacitate, reverse thrust below the hazardous level, thrust above maximum rated, loss of engine-support load-path integrity without separation, and significant uncontrollable thrust oscillation. Safety Lab Aero's severity rubric puts these three engine classes, not the aircraft five, in front of a Part 33 classification pass.`},{id:"certstd-50",source:"14 CFR §33.75(b)–(f) · FAA AC 33.75-1A ¶10–¶17, ¶21–¶22",topic:"§33.75 — critical parts, safety systems, maintenance and crew assumptions, the (f) list, and the accepted analysis methods",text:"The rest of §33.75 governs what the engine analysis may lean on. (b): the FAA may require failure-effect assumptions to be verified by test — AC ¶10: where the engineering judgment behind a predicted failure progression is doubtful, substantiate by service experience, analysis, material/component/rig/engine test. (c): the primary failure of certain single elements cannot be sensibly estimated numerically; where such a failure is likely to be hazardous, compliance is shown through the integrity requirements of §§33.15 (materials), 33.27 (rotor integrity) and 33.70 (engine life-limited parts), stated in the analysis — AC ¶8.c(2)–(3): disk, hub, impeller and large rotating-seal primary failures should be extremely remote but are NOT included in the per-effect summation. (d): a safety system relied on to stop a failure progressing (devices, instrumentation, early warning, maintenance checks, procedures) must have its own failure analyzed in combination with the basic engine failure — AC ¶12: as a latent failure, simultaneously, or subsequently — and reliability assumptions for parts outside the engine manufacturer's control go into the §33.5 installation instructions. (e): anything the analysis depends on must be identified and substantiated — (1) maintenance at stated intervals including verification of items that could fail latently, published in the §33.4 ICA when needed to prevent hazardous effects, with maintenance-error procedures in the manuals (AC ¶13.d lessons learned: multiple-engine maintenance by one crew in one visit — oil-system or borescope access not restored, misinstalled O-rings, wrong fluids — see SAIB NE-00-12; overlooked cracks and missing protective coatings on disks, hubs and spacers); (2) pre-flight or periodic verification of safety devices, published; (3) specific instrumentation not otherwise required; (4) flight-crew actions in the §33.5 operating instructions. (f): the analysis must at least cover indicating equipment, manual and automatic controls, compressor bleed, refrigerant injection, gas-temperature control, speed/power/thrust governors and fuel control, overspeed/overtemperature/topping limiters, propeller control, and thrust-reversal systems — and is not limited to them. AC ¶21: mitigate improper operation through operating instructions, and design or permanently mark parts whose incorrect assembly could be hazardous. AC ¶22: depth and scope follow complexity and criticality, severity, novelty and service experience, number of causal scenarios and detectability; accepted methods are the FMEA (inductive, bottom-up) and fault tree or dependence diagram / reliability block diagram (deductive, top-down; the fault tree failure-oriented, the dependence diagram success-oriented), with ARP4754A, ARP4761, AC 23.1309-1E, AC 25.1309-1A (now 1B), AC 27-1B, AC 29-2C and EASA CS-E 510 / AMC E 510 as references. Derivative engines may limit the analysis to modified components and their effects on the rest of the engine."},{id:"certstd-51",source:"14 CFR §35.15 (Amdt. 35-8, 73 FR 63346; 35-9; 35-9A) · Safety Lab Aero safety_targets.js",topic:"§35.15 propeller safety analysis — hazardous and major PROPELLER effects and the single probability criterion",text:`14 CFR §35.15, "Safety analysis," is the propeller analogue of §33.75. (a)(1): analyse the propeller system for the likely consequences of all reasonably expected failures, accounting for the typical installation (assumptions about representative components, interfaces or installed conditions stated), consequential secondary and dormant failures, and multiple failures. (a)(2): summarise failures that could cause major or hazardous propeller effects with estimated probabilities. (a)(3), verbatim criterion: "hazardous propeller effects are not predicted to occur at a rate in excess of that defined as extremely remote (probability of 10−7 or less per propeller flight hour)," with the same individual-failure alternative as the engine rule — not greater than 10^-8 per propeller flight hour — and the same reliance on engineering judgment and experience. Unlike §33.75(a)(4), §35.15 sets NO numeric criterion for major propeller effects; they are summarised and estimated only. (b): assumptions may have to be verified by test. (c): primary failures of single elements such as blades cannot be estimated numerically; if likely hazardous, those elements are propeller critical parts under §35.16. (d) and (e) mirror the engine rule (safety-system failure in combination, maintenance at stated intervals published in the §35.4 ICA, pre-flight verification, specific instrumentation) and add (e)(4) a fatigue assessment. (f): at least indicating equipment, manual and automatic controls, governors and propeller control systems, synchrophasers, synchronizers and thrust-reversal systems. (g)(1) hazardous propeller effects, verbatim: "(i) The development of excessive drag. (ii) A significant thrust in the opposite direction to that commanded by the pilot. (iii) The release of the propeller or any major portion of the propeller. (iv) A failure that results in excessive unbalance." (g)(2) major propeller effects for variable-pitch propellers: inability to feather (feathering propellers), inability to change pitch when commanded, a significant uncommanded change in pitch, a significant uncontrollable torque or speed fluctuation. Safety Lab Aero PROB_TARGETS "Part 35" carries Hazardous 1e-7 (the rule's per-effect figure), Major 1e-5 (the remote convention — §35.15 itself sets no major number), and Catastrophic 1e-9 / Minor 1e-3 as aircraft-level extensions. Both the Part 33 and Part 35 rows now carry Hazardous 1e-7, the per-effect summed criterion both rules set; the ≤1e-8 individual-failure route is carried in the severity rubric as the alternative, not in the scalar. (Asymmetry resolved by ruling 1 Sep 2026 — the Part 33 row previously held the 1e-8 individual-failure figure.)`},{id:"certstd-52",source:"FAA AC 20-174 (09/30/2011, AIR-120) ¶1, ¶3, ¶4",topic:"AC 20-174 — FAA recognition of ARP 4754A as the development-assurance process, and the two precedence rules",text:`FAA Advisory Circular 20-174, "Development of Civil Aircraft and Systems," dated 09/30/2011 (AIR-120), three pages, "recognizes the Society of Automotive Engineers (SAE) Aerospace Recommended Practice (ARP) 4754A, Guidelines for Development of Civil Aircraft and Systems, dated December 21, 2010, as an acceptable method for establishing a development assurance process" covering validation of requirements, verification of the design implementation, and process assurance. ¶1.b: the guidelines were developed in the Part 25 context and "may be applicable to other regulations, such as 14 CFR parts 23, 27, 29, 33, and 35"; ¶4.a lists the applicable sections as 23.1301/1309, 25.1301/1309, 27.1301/1309, 29.1301/1309, 33.28, 33.75, 35.23 and 35.15. ¶3 background: the AC addresses development errors in increasingly complex systems through the structured methodology of ARP 4754A, but "it is not intended to require that the more structured techniques described in ARP 4754A be applied where traditional techniques have been shown to be acceptable for more traditional systems designs." Guidance (second ¶3): coordinate use of ARP 4754A with the FAA early; propose and document in the certification plan the functional development assurance level (FDAL), item development assurance level (IDAL), the justification for the levels, and the ARP 4754A processes and objectives used — the plan requires FAA concurrence. Two PRECEDENCE rules matter for Safety Lab Aero's DAL allocation: ¶1.e — AC 23.1309-1 "may differ from and will take precedence over this AC with regards to development assurance levels," and ARP 4754A "should not be used to assign an assurance level lower than what is specified in AC 23.1309-1"; ¶3.c — "The FDAL and IDAL assignments in other ACs should take precedence over the application of SAE ARP 4754A, Section 5.2." So the cert-basis grid (AC 23.1309-1E Figure 2, AC 25.1309-1B, AC 29-2C Figure AC 29.1309-2, MOC VTOL.2510 Table 1) sets the FDAL floor, and ARP 4754A/B §5.2 architecture-based reduction (DALgebra) operates below it — never above it. Related ACs it cites: 20-115B (now 20-115D), 20-152 (now 20-152A), 20-170 (IMA / DO-297), 21-50, 23.1309-1, 25.1309-1, the 27/29 §1309 sections, 33.28-1 and 33.75-1. Note the tool's standards spine registers ARP4754B (2023); AC 20-174 names the 2010 ARP 4754A revision — cite the AC for FAA recognition and the current revision for method.`},{id:"certstd-53",source:"FAA AC 20-115D (07/21/2017, AIR-134) ¶1, ¶3, ¶4, ¶6",topic:"AC 20-115D — the FAA's software means of compliance: DO-178C/ED-12C, DO-330 tool qualification and the three supplements",text:`FAA Advisory Circular 20-115D, "Airborne Software Development Assurance Using EUROCAE ED-12( ) and RTCA DO-178( )," dated 07/21/2017 (AIR-134), cancels AC 20-115C of July 19, 2013, and is "an acceptable means, but not the only means, for showing compliance with the applicable airworthiness regulations for the software aspects of airborne systems and equipment in type certification or TSO authorization" — but "if you use the means described in the AC, you must follow it in all applicable respects." It recognises, as equivalent pairs: ED-12C / DO-178C (Software Considerations in Airborne Systems and Equipment Certification, January 2012 / December 13, 2011); ED-215 / DO-330 (Software Tool Qualification Considerations); ED-218 / DO-331 (Model-Based Development and Verification Supplement); ED-217 / DO-332 (Object-Oriented Technology and Related Techniques Supplement); ED-216 / DO-333 (Formal Methods Supplement); and names ED-94C / DO-248C (FAQs and discussion papers) as supporting information. ¶4: the recognised documents give objectives for the software life-cycle processes, activities that satisfy them, and descriptions of the evidence; the AC is harmonised with EASA AMC 20-115D. ¶6, using DO-178C: satisfy ALL objectives for the assigned software level and produce all associated life-cycle data per the Annex A tables (and the DO-330 / supplement Annex A tables where applicable); submit the section 9.3 data (and DO-330 section 9.0.a for tools) to the certification office; note that Design Description and Source Code are not type-design data for Level D (section 9.4); any FAA means of compliance for a specific regulation that states the relationship between system criticality and software level "will take precedence over the application of section 2.3 of ED-12C/DO-178C" — the same floor rule AC 20-174 states for FDAL/IDAL. ¶8: supplements cannot be used stand-alone; the PSAC must say how DO-178C and the supplements apply together and which objectives apply to which components; for model-based development, section MB.6.8.1 of DO-331 requires identifying which review/analysis objectives simulation satisfies and justifying each in detail. ¶8.b–c: field-loadable software must be protected against corruption or partial load to the integrity of its level, its part number verifiable once loaded, and field loading inhibited in flight; user-modifiable software's modifiable part is developed to at least the assigned level. Safety Lab Aero allocates the level (IDAL) from severity; DO-178C itself stays licensed and pointer-only.`},{id:"certstd-54",source:"FAA AC 20-115D ¶5, ¶9, ¶10, Table 1, Table 2",topic:"AC 20-115D — legacy DO-178B software, level equivalence, change impact analysis, and the DO-330 tool qualification levels",text:`AC 20-115D governs what happens to software approved under earlier revisions. ¶5: an applicant may keep using established ED-12B/DO-178B processes (including tool qualification) for NEW development only if all of these hold — no known process deficiencies (audits, reviews, open problem reports against DO-178B objectives, with closure evidence on request); the processes previously produced certified software at a level at least as high; model-based development, object-oriented technology or formal methods processes were already found acceptable by the FAA on a previous certified project; parameter-data-item (configuration data) processes were found acceptable, or new ones are set up per DO-178C; no significant change to the processes or environment; and no intent to declare DO-178C satisfied. Otherwise upgrade and use DO-178C; new life-cycle processes are built to DO-178C. ¶9 legacy software (DO-178, DO-178A, DO-178B): assess usage history — safety-related service difficulties, ADs, open problem reports — and fix related deficiencies first; "The system safety process assigns the minimum development assurance level based on the severity classifications of failure conditions for a given function"; DO-178B levels are consistent with DO-178C levels, while DO-178 (Critical / Essential / Non-Essential) and DO-178A (Levels 1 / 2 / 3) predate them, so Table 1 maps legacy levels to assigned levels — its own example: DO-178A Level 2 satisfies software Levels B, C and D but not A; DO-178 Essential software previously accepted as Level B remains acceptable. Unmodified legacy software may be approved on its original basis; modifications require a change impact analysis per DO-178C section 12.1, the verification it indicates, and a summary in the PSAC or SAS; equivalence to DO-178C may be declared for software (modified and unmodified) only after the baseline and all processes including tool qualification are upgraded to DO-178C and DO-330 — never for unmodified tools. ¶10 tool qualification: DO-178C section 12.2 and DO-330 are the method; DO-178C defines five tool qualification levels (TQL-1 to TQL-5, Table 12-1) from the tool's use and impact. Table 2 correlates DO-178B tool types to DO-330 TQLs: a DO-178B development tool at software Level A → criteria 1, TQL-1; Level B → TQL-2; Level C → TQL-3; Level D → TQL-4; a verification tool for Levels A or B → criteria 2, TQL-4; Levels C or D → criteria 2, TQL-5; any Level, criteria 3 → TQL-5. A previously qualified development tool may keep its DO-178B qualification only if its level meets the required TQL; a verification tool needing TQL-4 must be requalified under DO-330. Safety Lab Aero's FTA/FMEA tools are not DO-178C airborne software; this chunk is for classifying a CUSTOMER's software items and reuse claims, and for citing what the FAA will accept.`},{id:"certstd-55",source:"FAA AC 20-152A (10/7/22, AIR-622) ¶1–¶5",topic:"AC 20-152A — the FAA's AEH means of compliance: DO-254/ED-80 scope, DAL applicability, simple-versus-complex, and the custom-device objectives CD-1 to CD-12",text:`FAA Advisory Circular 20-152A, "Development Assurance for Airborne Electronic Hardware," dated 10/7/22 (AIR-622), cancels AC 20-152 of June 30, 2005. It recognises EUROCAE ED-80 (April 2000) and RTCA DO-254 (April 19, 2000), "Design Assurance Guidance for Airborne Electronic Hardware," as equivalent, and supplements them with objectives for custom devices (PLD, FPGA, ASIC — DO-254 section 1.2 item 3 "custom micro-coded components"), COTS intellectual property inside custom devices, COTS devices, and circuit board assemblies; objectives are identified CD-i, IP-i, COTS-i and CBA-i, and the applicant documents in the Plan for Hardware Aspects of Certification (PHAC) how each is satisfied. Applicability (¶2): AEH contributing to hardware DAL A, B or C functions; for DAL C only a limited set of objectives applies; "use of this AC is not required for AEH contributing to hardware DAL D functions" (AC 00-72 gives best practice); single-event effects are out of scope. ¶5.2 simple/complex: a custom device "is classified as simple only if a technical assessment of the design content supports the ability of the device to be verified by a comprehensive combination of deterministic tests and analyses that ensures correct functional performance under all foreseeable operating conditions with no anomalous behavior," judged on the number and simplicity of functions, interfaces and processing, independence of blocks, and for digital parts synchronous versus asynchronous design, number of clocks, and the number of state machines, states and transitions; anything not simple is complex, and a device built entirely of simple items may itself be complex. Objectives: CD-1 document each device's DAL, simple/complex classification and the justification; CD-2 a simple-device process covering function definition, complete verification by test and analysis, configuration management with problem reporting and reproduction instructions, and build-conformance assessment; CD-3 validate ALL custom-device requirements (derived and non-derived), with independence for DAL A and B; CD-4 detailed design review against design standards and traceability for DAL A/B, standards only for DAL C; CD-5 review synthesis and place-and-route tool reports; CD-6 review every verification case and procedure for correct, complete requirement coverage; CD-7 verify timing performance across temperature, supply and semiconductor process variation (static timing analysis is one means); CD-8 for DAL A/B define abnormal and boundary conditions and expected behaviour as requirements (robustness); CD-9 for DAL A/B HDL code coverage used for DO-254 Appendix B elemental analysis needs defined coverage criteria and justification of uncovered elements; CD-10 an independent assessment of a tool's output must justify sufficient coverage; CD-11 tool "relevant history" claims need data; CD-12 previously developed hardware is reassessed per DO-254 section 11.1 when modified, when its function, use or failure-condition classification rises, or when the design environment changes. ¶5.10 corrects DO-254 Appendix A Table A-1 rows (10.1.6, 10.2.2, 10.3.2.2, 10.4.2) and equates the Top-Level Drawing to a Hardware Configuration Index (HCI) plus HECI. DO-254 itself stays licensed and pointer-only in Safety Lab Aero.`},{id:"certstd-56",source:"FAA AC 20-152A ¶5.11, ¶6, ¶7",topic:"AC 20-152A — COTS IP, COTS devices and circuit board assemblies: the objectives that feed the system safety assessment",text:`The rest of AC 20-152A covers the hardware nobody develops from scratch. ¶5.11 COTS intellectual property instantiated in FPGAs/PLDs/ASICs (soft, firm or hard IP inserted by the applicant — hard IP embedded in the silicon by the FPGA vendor is treated as part of the COTS device under ¶6): the risks are missing behavioural or integration documentation, insufficient vendor verification, poor quality, misalignment between the vendor's intended usage and the applicant's, and errors the integrator introduces while completing the physical implementation without knowing the IP's internals. Objectives: IP-1 select IP that is technically suitable, architecturally understood, documented well enough to integrate and verify (datasheets, application notes, errata), implementable (synthesis constraints, limits, routing) and demonstrably fulfils its function; IP-2 assess the provider and data — integration information, documented configurations and options, verification by a trustworthy process covering the applicant's use case, known errors with an update process, and service experience for the use case — documented and submitted; IP-3 where IP-2 criteria 1, 2, 4 or 5 are not met, define complementary DO-254-based development assurance; and a verification strategy covering the IP itself, its implementation (no errors introduced by synthesis/place-and-route) and its integration (connection, configuration, constraints). ¶6 COTS devices: COTS-1 assess and document the complexity of COTS devices with rationale; an electronic component management process; COTS-3 establish reliability and suitability when a complex COTS device is used outside its datasheet limits; COTS-4 a means of compliance for embedded microcode that is not vendor-qualified or is modified; COTS-5 assess relevant errata and identify and verify mitigations, feeding non-hardware mitigations back to the appropriate process; COTS-6 "identify the failure modes of the used functions of the device and the possible associated common modes, and feed both of these back to the system safety assessment process" — the hook into ARP4761A FMEA/CMA that Safety Lab Aero's item-level lanes expect; COTS-8 for DAL A/B functions, develop and verify mitigation against inadvertent alteration of the device's critical configuration settings. ¶7 circuit board assemblies: development assurance for the CBA as an item (CBA-i objectives), applicable when the assembly contributes to DAL A, B or C functions. Posture: AC text is public domain and quoted; DO-254/ED-80 are RTCA/EUROCAE licensed — cite and point.`},{id:"certstd-57",source:"EASA CS 25.1309 (ED Decision 2020/001/R, Amdt 25/24) · AMC 25.1309 (ED Decision 2021/015/R) §7–§8",topic:"EASA CS 25.1309 and AMC 25.1309 — the rule, the classes, the ranges, and where EASA differs from AC 25.1309-1B",text:'EASA CS 25.1309, Equipment, systems and installations (ED Decision 2020/001/R, amendments 25/5, 25/6, 25/19, 25/20, 25/24), is the European twin of 14 CFR §25.1309 and reads to the same structure: (a) required equipment performs as intended under the operating and environmental conditions and other equipment is not a source of danger; (b)(1) any catastrophic failure condition is extremely improbable AND does not result from a single failure; (b)(2) hazardous extremely remote; (b)(3) major remote; (b)(4) significant latent failures eliminated as far as practical or their latency minimised; (b)(5) for a catastrophic condition resulting from two failures, either latent for more than one flight: additional redundancy impractical, the condition remote given a single latent failure, and the sum of the latent-failure probabilities combined with each evident failure not exceeding 1/1 000; (c) crew information for unsafe system operating conditions and design to minimise crew error (CS 25.1302); (d) EWIS assessed per CS 25.1709; (e) Certification Maintenance Requirements in the ALS (CS 25.1529). Exceptions named in the rule: flight-control jams under CS 25.671(c)(3) from (b)(1)(ii); certain CS 25.735(b) single failures, CS 25.810 and CS 25.812 conditions from (b); powerplant installations per CS 25.901(c). AMC 25.1309, System design and analysis (ED Decision 2021/015/R), §7.a classifies No Safety Effect / Minor / Major / Hazardous / Catastrophic with the same substance as AC 25.1309-1B §3.1 (Hazardous: serious or fatal injury to a relatively small number of occupants other than the flight crew; Catastrophic: multiple fatalities, usually with loss of the aeroplane; loss of continued safe flight and landing is catastrophic unless another AMC defines otherwise, and for flight controls CS&FL is defined in AMC 25.671 ¶4 and ¶7). §7.b qualitative terms match the FAA. §7.c quantitative ranges, average probability per flight hour: Probable — greater than the order of 1×10^-5 (EASA states no 10^-3 upper bound here); Remote — 10^-5 or less down to 10^-7; Extremely Remote — 10^-7 down to 10^-9; Extremely Improbable — 10^-9 or less. §8 Figure 2b gives the allowable quantitative probabilities per class as <10^-3 (Minor, reference only, Note 1), <10^-5 (Major), <10^-7 (Hazardous), <10^-9 (Catastrophic); Figure 2a is the same three-row effects table as FAA Table 4-1 (aeroplane / occupants excluding flight crew / flight crew). §8.c: the catastrophic objective is met by no single failure, extremely improbable, AND — new with (b)(5) — remote given a single latent failure. Safety Lab Aero PROB_TARGETS "Part 25" (1e-9 / 1e-7 / 1e-5 / 1e-3) therefore serves a CS-25 program unchanged; the cert-basis picker maps CS-25 onto the Part 25 row and the spine cites CS 25.1309 as the EU equivalent.'},{id:"certstd-58",source:"EASA AMC 25.1309 §9.b(4), §9.b(6), §11.b, Appendix 5",topic:"AMC 25.1309 — development assurance with architecture credit, the 1/1 000 latency rule with the worst-case-flight and P = λT ≤ 0.1 conventions, and the single-failure rule",text:"Three places where AMC 25.1309 says something the FAA AC says differently or not at all. Development assurance, §9.b(4): the FDAL/IDAL level should be commensurate with the severity of the failure conditions the system contributes to, ARP 4754A/ED-79A (the document referenced in AMC §3.b(2)) gives the assignment guidelines, and EASA explicitly recognizes that credit can be taken from system architecture — functional or item development independence — in the FDAL/IDAL assignment process; software assurance follows the ED-12/DO-178 document in §3.a(3); and, as of this AMC revision, EASA states there is currently no agreed development assurance standard for airborne electronic hardware (the FAA, by contrast, recognizes DO-254 via AC 20-152A; EASA uses AMC 20-152A). Significant latent failures, §9.b(6): (b)(4) is a hierarchy — eliminate SLFs to the maximum practical extent with state-of-the-art monitoring and crew indication (AMC 25-19 Section 8), then minimize exposure by design with pilot- or self-initiated checks rather than long scheduled-maintenance intervals, then, if scheduled tasks are relied on, address latency quantitatively and qualitatively (AMC 25-19 Section 10, CCMRs); (b)(5) applies to the remaining dual-failure catastrophic conditions with one failure latent more than one flight — highlight them to EASA early with rationale why avoidance is impractical; two criteria: limit latency (sum of latent-failure probabilities combined with each evident failure ≤ 1/1 000, applied per catastrophic condition, singly or summed when an evident failure appears in several dual combinations) and limit residual probability (the combined average probability per flight hour of all single active failures completing the condition, given one latent failure, must be remote); the latent probability is derived from the worst-case flight — the evident failure on the last flight before the scheduled inspection, the latent failure anywhere in the interval — and with constant failure rates may be computed as exposure time × failure rate when that product is ≤ 0.1. Appendix 5 works the minimal-cut-set method: list cut sets by order, pick the dual-order sets containing a latent event, group by common active event (sum latents < 1/1 000) and by common latent event (sum actives, remote), and the total for (b)(1). Single failure, §11.b: identical to the FAA — a single failure includes any set of failures not shown independent; common-cause, common-mode and cascading failures are dependent from the root cause; failure containment by partitioning, separation and isolation; and a failure mode may be discounted only when any knowledgeable person would conclude it simply would not occur — with EASA's own example that a simply loaded static element meeting CS 25.571 damage tolerance need not be assumed to fracture by fatigue. Safety Lab Aero's FTA latency/CSL+1 checks implement the same 1/1 000 and remote (≤1e-5) criteria for both authorities."},{id:"certstd-59",source:"EASA AMC 25.1309 §9.c, §10, §11.a, Appendix 4 (ED Decision 2020/001/R)",topic:"AMC 25.1309 — annunciation loss, FHA and severity factors, and the EASA Appendix 4 allowable probabilities (sparser than FAA Appendix E)",text:"AMC 25.1309 §9.c on CS 25.1309(c): an unsafe system operating condition is any condition that, undetected and unaccommodated by the crew, would contribute to or cause a hazardous or catastrophic failure condition; information takes the form of a warning (immediate recognition and action), a caution (immediate awareness, later action), an advisory (awareness, action may be needed) or a message, per CS 25.1322 / AMC 25.1322; a failure-monitoring system's reliability must match the objective of the function it monitors — a catastrophic un-annunciated effect makes the system-plus-annunciation combination extremely improbable — and loss of annunciation is itself a failure condition (the FAA AC additionally presumes it major unless shown otherwise; the AMC asks for particular attention to crew ability to cope); nuisance warnings are assessed; periodic maintenance or crew checks to detect SLFs are undesirable in lieu of monitoring. §10 identification and assessment: airplane-level and system-level FHAs, combinations through common-cause analyses, minor-or-major conditions that become hazardous in combination; the FHA is systematic and comprehensive, performed early, updated, and used to set the development assurance levels; severity is evaluated by effects on the airplane, on crew members and on occupants, with flight duration, flight phase and diversion time as intensifying factors where they worsen the outcome, weather and adverse conditions intensifying, and unaffected similar functions or crew recognition and action alleviating — subject to sufficient detection means and timeliness; combinations of factors only if anticipated together. §11.a: classification is necessarily qualitative, probability assessment qualitative or quantitative, depth by function type, severity and complexity; software and hardware complexity need not dominate system-level complexity. Appendix 4 allowable probabilities (ED Decision 2020/001/R, Amdt 25/24) — the EASA table is SPARSER than FAA AC 25.1309-1B Appendix E: CS-25 Appendix C icing 1; Appendix O icing 10^-2 per flight hour; severe icing beyond certified conditions no accepted standard data; head wind >25 kt, tail wind >10 kt and cross wind >20 kt at takeoff/landing each 10^-2 per flight (AC 120-28 / CS-AWO, without the NLR alternative values the FAA lists); limit gust and turbulence 10^-5 per flight hour (CS 25.341); air temperature below -70 °C no data; CG and weights uniform over the approved range; flight condition requiring stall warning 10^-2 per flight and a stall 10^-5 per flight (assumptions); VMO/MMO exceedance 10^-2 per flight; ≥1.5 g and ≤0 g no data; and — unlike the FAA — any rejected take-off, high-energy rejected take-off, fuel jettison, go-around, lavatory fire and cargo-compartment fire all carry no accepted standard data, so a European applicant must justify any value below 1 for those. Both authorities: where no accepted data exists, a probability of 1 applies unless justified, and the environmental condition must be independent of the system failure."},{id:"certstd-60",source:"EASA CS-23 Amendment 6 / AMC & GM Issue 5 (ED Decisions 2017/013/R, 2023/002/R, 2026/003/R) — CS 23.2005, CS 23.2510, AMC1/AMC2/AMC3 23.2510, GM3 23.2010",topic:"EASA CS-23 — certification LEVELS by seats, CS 23.2510, and the ASTM F44 standards EASA accepts (with its variances)",text:"EASA CS-23 (normal-category aeroplanes) has been performance-based since Amendment 5 (ED Decision 2017/013/R, NPA 2016-05) — the old prescriptive CS 23.1301–23.1461 were deleted and moved to AMC, and CS 23.2500–23.2555 created; the current Easy Access Rules are CS Amendment 6 with AMC & GM Issue 5 (ED Decision 2026/003/R, May 2026). CS 23.2005: normal category covers up to 19 passenger seats and 8 618 kg (19 000 lb) MCTOM; certification LEVELS are by passenger seating — Level 1 (0–1), Level 2 (2–6), Level 3 (7–9), Level 4 (10–19) — and performance levels are Low speed (VNO or VMO ≤ 250 KCAS or MMO ≤ 0.6) and High speed. Note the difference from the FAA advisory scheme: AC 23.1309-1E Classes I–IV are keyed on engine type and weight, while CS 23.2005 (and 14 CFR §23.2005 since amendment 23-64) Levels 1–4 are keyed on seats; Safety Lab Aero's Part 23 rows use the AC classes because that is where the published numbers are. CS 23.2510, Equipment, systems, and installations (ED Decision 2017/013/R), is word-for-word the §23.2510 objective: (a) each catastrophic failure condition extremely improbable, each hazardous extremely remote, each major remote, for the equipment identified in CS 23.2500 considered separately and in relation to other systems; (b) equipment not covered by CS 23.2500 must not cause a hazard. AMC1 23.2510 (ED Decision 2026/003/R) accepts ASTM F3264 section 9.3 and the F44 standards F3233/F3233M-23b (flight and navigation instrumentation), F3309/F3309M-24a (Simplified Safety Assessment) and F3408/F3408M-21 (emergency parachute recovery), with EASA variances stated in its Remarks: against F3061/F3061M-24a paragraph 4.4.2, system-level verification is usually not acceptable and should be discussed with EASA; for aircraft powered by electric motors, EASA has not yet accepted Table 3 of F3230-25 (the safety-assessment probability table) and its use should be discussed with EASA; and F3227-25 does not completely cover oxygen fire hazards. AMC2 23.2510 accepts the legacy prescriptive route — CS-23 Amendment 4 paragraphs 23.1309, 23.1323, 23.1325, 23.1329, 23.1331, 23.1335, 23.1337, 23.1357, 23.1431, 23.1437, 23.672(c), 23.677(d), 23.701, 23.735(d), 23.775(g), 23.831(d), 23.841 — and AMC3 23.2510 the CS-VLA Amendment 1 equivalents. GM3 23.2010 tabulates every accepted ASTM F44 standard with the revision accepted at Issue 5 versus Issue 4, including F3061/F3061M-24a (was 20), F3230-25 (was 20a), F3309/F3309M-24a (was 21), F3232/F3232M-25 (flight controls), F3236-21a (HIRF), F3367-23 (simplified HIRF/IEL) and F3532-25 (protection from intentional unauthorised electronic interactions). The ASTM standards themselves are licensed — cite and point; the objective numbers for a CS-23 level-based program live in F3230 Table 3 and are not reproduced here."},{id:"certstd-61",source:"EASA CS-27 Amendment 10 (Feb 2023) — CS 27.1309 (ED Decision 2023/001/R) · AMC1 27.1309 Table 1 and Table 2 · Safety Lab Aero safety_targets.js",topic:"EASA AMC1 27.1309 — the PUBLISHED four-class small-rotorcraft grid that verifies the Part 27 numbers (with EASA's class definitions)",text:`EASA CS 27.1309 (ED Decision 2023/001/R, Amdt 27/4) reads like the FAA rule: (b)(1) each catastrophic failure condition extremely improbable and not from a single failure; (b)(2) hazardous extremely remote; (b)(3) major remote; (d) timely, clear information on unsafe system operating conditions. AMC1 27.1309 states that the AMC to CS-27 is FAA AC 27-1B Change 7 (4 February 2016) and that this AMC identifies only the differences — in particular it introduces FOUR CLASSES of CS-27 rotorcraft for proportionality, takes precedence where stipulated, and sends Class IV to AMC 29.1309. Table 1, class definitions (EASA): Class IV — rotorcraft Category A; Class III — Category B with 6 or more occupants including crew, or above 1 814 kg (4 000 lb) maximum gross weight; Class II — Category B limited to 5 occupants including crew and 1 814 kg; Class I — Category B limited to 2 occupants including crew and 1 814 kg, VFR only (day and night); a rotorcraft intended for IFR must be at least Class II. Table 2, safety objectives per flight hour with FDAL (for use with ED-79A/ARP4754A and ARP4761): Class I — Minor ≤10^-3 FDAL D, Major ≤10^-4 FDAL C, Hazardous ≤10^-5 FDAL C, Catastrophic ≤10^-6 FDAL C; Class II — 10^-3 D, 10^-5 C, 10^-6 C, 10^-7 C; Class III — 10^-3 D, 10^-5 C, 10^-7 C, 10^-8 B; Class IV — 10^-3 D, 10^-5 C, 10^-7 B, 10^-9 A. Note 1: no quantitative analysis expected for minor. Note 2: per flight hour, with an average flight profile and duration defined, and failure-rate uncertainty acknowledged. The FDALs are a correlation to the class, not a probability. VERIFICATION (1 Sep 2026): this table is IDENTICAL, cell for cell, to the 2017 FAA/EASA symposium draft of PS-ASW-27-15 and to Safety Lab Aero's PROB_TARGETS/DAL_TARGETS "Part 27 I" through "Part 27 IV" — so the NUMBERS in the engine are now verified against a published, in-force regulatory text (EASA), while the FAA class DEFINITIONS (reciprocating / single turbine ≤4 000 lb / single turbine 4 001–7 000 lb / twin turbine) remain those of the 2017 draft because the final PS-ASW-27-15 text was not obtained. A CS-27 applicant uses Table 1 to pick the class; an FAA Part 27 applicant uses the PS-ASW-27-15 definitions; both land on the same four rows. Applicability notes mirror AC 29-2C: CS 27.1309 does not apply to the uninstalled engine or the rotor drive system itself but does apply to their installed equipment (cooling, lubrication, chip detection, rotor brake, VHM), nor to stowed safety equipment, non-safety equipment or lighting characteristics — only to the hazards those installations introduce.`},{id:"certstd-62",source:"EASA CS-29 Amendment 12 (Jul 2026) — CS 29.1309 · AMC1 29.1309 (ED Decision 2023/001/R)",topic:"EASA CS 29.1309 / AMC1 29.1309 — Category A loss of CS&FL is catastrophic by rule; the AMC is FAA AC 29-2C Chg 7 plus EASA development-assurance recognitions",text:"EASA CS 29.1309 (ED Decision 2023/001/R, Amdt 29/4 and 29/11) carries the same three objectives as CS 27.1309 with one addition written into the rule at (b)(1): for Category A rotorcraft, the occurrence of any failure condition which would prevent the continued safe flight and landing of the rotorcraft is considered catastrophic. (a) intended function throughout the certified operating and environmental conditions; (c) uncovered equipment must not cause a hazard; (d) timely, clear unsafe-condition information to the responsible flight crew member. AMC1 29.1309: the AMC to CS-29 is FAA AC 29-2C Change 7 (4 February 2016), and AMC 29.1309 supplements it and takes precedence where stipulated. Its single-failure and common-cause paragraph is the AMC 25.1309 wording (a single failure includes any set of failures not shown independent; common-cause, common-mode and cascading failures are dependent from the root cause; development, manufacturing, installation and maintenance errors are common-cause sources; ARP4761 for PRA/ZSA/CMA; a single-failure catastrophe may be judged not a practical possibility only when the logic is straightforward and obvious), and it allows periodic maintenance or flight-crew checks to limit exposure to significant latent failures. Development assurance: ED-79A/ARP4754A recognized for aircraft and systems down to the software/AEH boundary, with extent depending on complexity and integration, including modifications; AMC 20-115 recognized for software against CS 29.1309(a), (b) and (c); AMC 20-152 for AEH; AMC 20-189 for open problem reports; AMC 20-170 for integrated modular avionics. Numbers: none restated in the AMC beyond the AC 29-2C Figure AC 29.1309-2 it adopts — Minor ≤10^-3 / D, Major ≤10^-5 / C, Hazardous ≤10^-7 / B, Catastrophic ≤10^-9 / A, the same as Safety Lab Aero's Part 29 row, so a CS-29 program maps onto it unchanged. CS 29.1305(a)(4)(ii) / AMC1 29.1305 and the fuel-quantity/low-level sensor independence guidance show EASA's expectation that particular-risk and zonal analyses demonstrate no common mode where sensors share structure."},{id:"certstd-63",source:"EASA CS-E Amendment 5 (Feb 2020) — CS-E 510 Safety analysis (Amdt E/1) · AMC E 510 (ED Decision 2015/009/R)",topic:"EASA CS-E 510 — the engine safety analysis rule and AMC that mirror §33.75 / AC 33.75-1A, with CS-E 515 critical parts and CS-E 30 assumptions",text:"EASA CS-E 510, Safety analysis (Amendment E/1; Easy Access Rules for Engines CS-E Amendment 5, February 2020), is the European twin of 14 CFR §33.75 and uses the same three engine-level effect classes. (a)(3): Hazardous Engine Effects predicted at a rate not in excess of Extremely Remote — stated as a probability LESS THAN 10^-7 per engine flight hour (the FAA rule states the 10^-7 to 10^-9 range) — with the same alternative that, because individual-failure estimates may be too imprecise to sum, the intent is met if the probability of a Hazardous Engine Effect from an individual failure is not greater than 10^-8 per engine flight hour (see CS-E 510(c)). (a)(4): Major Engine Effects at a rate not in excess of Remote, less than 10^-5 per engine flight hour. (b) assumptions may be verified by test; (c) primary failures of certain single elements cannot be estimated numerically — reliance on the integrity specifications of CS-E 515 (Engine Critical Parts), stated in the analysis; (d) safety-system failure in combination with a basic engine failure, with assumptions for items outside the applicant's control identified per CS-E 30; (e) maintenance actions at stated intervals (published in the airworthiness limitations section under CS-E 25 when needed to keep hazardous effects below Extremely Remote), pre-flight verification of safety devices, specific instrumentation, and flight-crew actions in the CS-E 20(d) operating instructions; (f) at least indicating equipment, aircraft-supplied data or electrical power (an item the FAA list does not name), compressor bleed, refrigerant injection, gas-temperature control, governors and fuel control, over-speed/over-temperature/topping limiters, propeller control and thrust reversal. (g) the failure definitions are the §33.75(g) set: minor = partial or complete loss of thrust or power and associated services; the seven hazardous effects (non-containment of high-energy debris; toxic bleed-air concentration sufficient to incapacitate; significant thrust opposite to that commanded; uncontrolled fire; engine-mount failure leading to separation; propeller release; complete inability to shut down); major = between the two. AMC E 510 (ED Decision 2015/009/R) restates AC 33.75-1A almost verbatim: aircraft-level classifications are not directly applicable to engine assessments; the CS-E 50 control-system requirements are integrated into the safety analysis; the 10^-7 per-effect target is a SUMMATION over individual failure modes and combinations excluding Engine Critical Parts (discs, hubs, spacers), dormant periods included, with no summation needed when every contributor is below 10^-8; critical-part primary LCF failure is supported by CS-E 515 rather than a number; discs, hubs, impellers and large rotating seals always represent high-energy debris and high-pressure casings may; the toxic-products, reverse-thrust, uncontrolled-fire and inability-to-shut-down scope rules match the FAA AC; major effects at ≤10^-5 each without summation, with the same guide list (controlled fires, contained case burn-through, low-energy parts, vibration causing crew discomfort, toxic bleed that degrades but does not incapacitate, sub-hazardous reverse thrust, loss of mount load path without separation, thrust above maximum rated, uncontrollable thrust oscillation). Safety Lab Aero's Part 33 row (Hazardous 1e-7 per-effect, Major 1e-5) is therefore consistent with CS-E 510 as well as §33.75; the per-effect summed criterion for both authorities is 10^-7, and the ≤1e-8 individual-failure route is carried in the rubric."},{id:"certstd-64",source:"14 CFR §21.16 · §21.17 (Subpart B) · FAA AC 21-101",topic:"Part 21 — designating the applicable regulations, special conditions, and the §21.17(b) special-class route that SC-VTOL uses",text:`14 CFR Part 21, "Certification Procedures for Products and Articles," is where the certification BASIS is set — the thing the cert-basis spine exists to model. §21.17 Designation of applicable regulations: (a) a type-certificate applicant must show the product meets the applicable requirements of the subchapter effective on the date of application (unless the FAA specifies otherwise or later amendments are elected or required) plus "Any special conditions prescribed by the FAA"; (b), verbatim, is the route every nonconventional design travels: "For special classes of aircraft, including the engines and propellers installed thereon (e.g., gliders, airships, and other nonconventional aircraft), for which airworthiness standards have not been issued under this subchapter, the applicable requirements will be the portions of those other airworthiness requirements contained in Parts 23, 25, 27, 29, 31, 33, and 35 found by the FAA to be appropriate for the aircraft and applicable to a specific type design, or such airworthiness criteria as the FAA may find provide an equivalent level of safety to those parts." (c) a transport-category application is effective for 5 years, others 3 years. That §21.17(b) special-class mechanism, with special conditions, is exactly how a powered-lift / eVTOL programme obtains its basis (EASA does the parallel thing with its Special Condition SC-VTOL). §21.16 Special conditions, verbatim: "If the FAA finds that the airworthiness regulations of this subchapter do not contain adequate or appropriate safety standards for an aircraft, aircraft engine, or propeller because of a novel or unusual design feature ... he prescribes special conditions ... [that] contain such safety standards ... as the FAA finds necessary to establish a level of safety equivalent to that established in the regulations." Safety Lab Aero's spine already carries §21.16, §21.17, §21.101 and the FAA Issue Paper process as Part 21 clauses and AC 21-101 as advisory; this chunk lets ANEM explain, in free-text chat, how a §__.1309 system-safety showing sits inside the §21.17 basis rather than treating the basis as given.`},{id:"certstd-65",source:"14 CFR §21.101 (Subpart D — Changed Product Rule) · FAA AC 21.101",topic:'Part 21 — the changed product rule (§21.101), the "areas affected by the change" test, and where §__.1309 re-opens on a modification',text:`14 CFR §21.101, "Designation of applicable regulations" (the Changed Product Rule, CPR — the basis rule for STCs and amended type certificates), verbatim opening: "(a) An applicant for a change to a type certificate must show that the change and areas affected by the change comply with the airworthiness requirements applicable to the category of the product in effect on the date of the application for the change and with parts 34, 36, and 38 of this chapter." (b) allows compliance with an EARLIER amendment for the change and areas affected by it in defined cases (a change not significant; an area, system, component, equipment or appliance the FAA finds the later amendment would not contribute materially to the level of safety or would be impractical for; the earlier amendment may not precede the one included by reference in the type certificate). The engineering consequence Safety Lab Aero cares about: a modification re-opens §__.1309 (and its AC/AMC and the ARP4754A/4761A safety-assessment evidence) for the change AND every "area affected by the change" — the same trigger AC 25.1309-1B chapter 9 and AC 20-152A objective CD-12 (previously developed hardware) describe from the assurance side. FAA AC 21.101 gives the method for identifying substantial change and the affected areas. This is why the tool's safety assessment must be scoped to the change set, not the whole aircraft, on a derivative programme — and why a stored SSA that predates a change cannot simply be reused without a change-impact analysis.`},{id:"certstd-66",source:"14 CFR Part 91 / Part 121 / Part 135 (operating rules) · §__.1309(a)",topic:'Operating rules vs airworthiness standards — why Part 91/121/135 are the "required by operating rules" trigger, not a system-safety ladder',text:`Part 91 (General Operating and Flight Rules), Part 121 (air carriers) and Part 135 (commuter and on-demand) are OPERATING rules — they govern how an already-certificated aircraft is flown and maintained, not how its type design is certified. They are relevant to the safety assessment in exactly one, specific way: every §__.1309(a) rule (25.1309(a), 23.2510 via 23.2500, 27.1309, 29.1309, and the SC-VTOL VTOL.2500) applies to equipment "required ... by operating rules" — so when an operating rule (e.g. Part 91 IFR equipment, Part 135 terrain awareness, Part 121 flight recorders) mandates a system, that system is pulled into the §__.1309 airworthiness showing and must meet the failure-condition objectives for its severity. The operating rules themselves carry no failure-condition severity ladder and no per-flight-hour probability targets, so there is nothing to add to PROB_TARGETS/DAL_TARGETS for them; they are cited as the SOURCE of a required function, and the severity of that function's loss is then classified under the aircraft's airworthiness basis. Part 21, by contrast, is a certification-PROCEDURE part and does bear on the basis (see the §21.17 / §21.101 chunks). Safety Lab Aero models the airworthiness basis (Part 23/25/27/29/33/35, SC-VTOL) and the Part 21 machinery that sets it; it treats operating-rule citations as function-requirement provenance, not as a separate severity scale.`}];typeof window<"u"&&(window.SL_CERTSTD_KB={version:11,method:"lexical",source:SRC,chunks}),typeof module<"u"&&(module.exports={chunks})})();
